1
00:00:13,303 --> 00:00:21,110
[SPEAKER_00]: One can pervade the body with rapture, because it's very, very even faster.

2
00:00:23,119 --> 00:00:25,842
[SPEAKER_00]: The important thing is to know that it's on the level of intention.

3
00:00:25,882 --> 00:00:28,966
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's on the level of your mind, not on the level of

4
00:00:42,788 --> 00:00:47,209
[SPEAKER_01]: Observation of physical sensations, or body sweeping, or whatever else people might do.

5
00:00:47,750 --> 00:00:48,010
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

6
00:00:48,130 --> 00:00:54,191
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, all of those little things that you're kind of trying to patch up, they're all implying this intention of safety.

7
00:00:54,211 --> 00:01:00,813
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you're not seeing the intention to pervade your body with rapture, none of these things can actually bring any understanding.

8
00:01:01,134 --> 00:01:02,474
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's that's the important thing.

9
00:01:02,494 --> 00:01:03,574
[SPEAKER_01]: You want to develop understanding.

10
00:01:03,594 --> 00:01:11,757
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't want to just keep having to go through body sweeping, attention, relaxing, relaxation technique, shavasanas, or whatever else.

11
00:01:12,477 --> 00:01:23,081
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, again, that's all... well, it's aiming at the wrong thing, and that wrong thing might sometimes imply the correct intention, but you don't even know what correct intention is, which means you won't discern it.

12
00:01:25,422 --> 00:01:35,365
[SPEAKER_01]: So, purveying your body with rapture, or relaxing your body, it's done to the body as a unit, as a whole, through that general intention of relaxing.

13
00:01:36,461 --> 00:01:40,444
[SPEAKER_01]: And you can even relax yourself by trying to understand what the relaxing is, as a phenomenon.

14
00:01:40,945 --> 00:01:43,206
[SPEAKER_01]: Just as I say, relaxing, relax... What is relaxing?

15
00:01:43,967 --> 00:01:48,331
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, if somebody tells me to relax, and I want to relax, what would... what do I think?

16
00:01:48,611 --> 00:01:50,112
[SPEAKER_01]: What is the attitude that I have?

17
00:01:50,652 --> 00:01:52,694
[SPEAKER_01]: And that attitude, basically, it's an attitude.

18
00:01:53,134 --> 00:01:58,439
[SPEAKER_01]: So, words, from attitude of the mind in regard to the body as a whole.

19
00:01:58,999 --> 00:02:03,663
[SPEAKER_01]: Not just, as I said before, toward your elbows or shoulders or whatever else.

20
00:02:03,863 --> 00:02:04,524
[SPEAKER_01]: As a whole.

21
00:02:08,436 --> 00:02:09,277
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's all you can say.

22
00:02:09,297 --> 00:02:10,177
[SPEAKER_01]: It is.

23
00:02:10,457 --> 00:02:15,200
[SPEAKER_01]: All of that, all of that, all of the other things people, people don't account for, it's just because they don't really know where to look.

24
00:02:15,680 --> 00:02:22,565
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, all these things might sometimes induce the the genuine relaxing, the genuine rapture.

25
00:02:22,745 --> 00:02:25,586
[SPEAKER_01]: But they don't know why or how, which means it's not understanding.

26
00:02:25,647 --> 00:02:30,009
[SPEAKER_01]: So, they just stumble upon it when it comes, and they don't, they wouldn't know how to do it again.

27
00:02:31,970 --> 00:02:36,133
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, when people say, oh, in the suttas, oh, I have taken jhānas at will.

28
00:02:37,698 --> 00:02:44,541
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that's done through understanding, through full understanding of jhānas up, and then you just say, yes, you know, but they don't come by chance.

29
00:02:47,302 --> 00:02:49,863
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, jhānas is a particular attitude.

30
00:02:50,524 --> 00:02:55,386
[SPEAKER_01]: Particular establishment of mind in regard for bhagavad-gītā.

31
00:02:55,466 --> 00:02:59,147
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you keep refining that, but it's the same, it's the same principle.

32
00:03:02,409 --> 00:03:04,730
[SPEAKER_00]: But you've got to be able to notice that general attitude.

33
00:03:05,592 --> 00:03:09,714
[SPEAKER_00]: Whatever it is, you want relaxation at some point.

34
00:03:09,734 --> 00:03:12,776
[SPEAKER_00]: You've got to be able to, oh, I know how to relax.

35
00:03:12,976 --> 00:03:13,796
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, exactly, yeah.

36
00:03:14,036 --> 00:03:17,478
[SPEAKER_01]: And you will know how to relax by trying to find out how to relax.

37
00:03:18,339 --> 00:03:18,539
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

38
00:03:19,179 --> 00:03:30,965
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, find out in terms of discerning the nature of what an intention to relax is at a time, not just by reading about some, you know, I don't know, relaxation self-help books and stuff like that.

39
00:03:36,105 --> 00:03:37,586
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if I tell you, just listen.

40
00:03:37,686 --> 00:03:39,106
[SPEAKER_01]: Can you just relax now?

41
00:03:39,146 --> 00:03:40,026
[SPEAKER_01]: Just sit and relax.

42
00:03:41,607 --> 00:03:45,788
[SPEAKER_01]: You can kind of let it sort of go quickly, whatever, but if you stop and think about it, what would you do?

43
00:03:47,409 --> 00:03:48,229
[SPEAKER_01]: What would you intend?

44
00:03:48,469 --> 00:03:57,032
[SPEAKER_01]: What kind of attitude would you arouse in regard to your present situation that is sort of rooted in your body?

45
00:03:58,092 --> 00:03:59,953
[SPEAKER_01]: If you want to relax, if you want to relax.

46
00:04:01,994 --> 00:04:04,296
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you could describe it in different ways, then.

47
00:04:04,376 --> 00:04:14,562
[SPEAKER_01]: You could say, uppervailing with rapture, or you can say, like, what the Ānāpānasati Siddha says, calming down the bodily determinations.

48
00:04:14,582 --> 00:04:26,009
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the bodily saṅkharas, basically, things upon which... sorry, bodily things upon which other things depend.

49
00:04:29,932 --> 00:04:30,913
[SPEAKER_01]: But you don't, again,

50
00:04:31,731 --> 00:04:33,612
[SPEAKER_01]: Sankaras are always plural in that sense.

51
00:04:35,133 --> 00:04:40,035
[SPEAKER_01]: But what you can do is recognize that plurality as a single phenomenon.

52
00:04:40,516 --> 00:04:46,499
[SPEAKER_01]: So, your body as a whole has all sorts of manifestations, all sorts of intentionality that pertains to that.

53
00:04:47,379 --> 00:04:53,883
[SPEAKER_01]: So, all you do basically is, ah, you count that whole down and all of these particular things are counted as a result.

54
00:04:55,604 --> 00:04:59,706
[SPEAKER_01]: If you have the intention of counting down your body on that general level,

55
00:05:00,965 --> 00:05:06,868
[SPEAKER_01]: On a mental level, then, whatever you do with that body cannot be not caught, if you're genuinely caught.

56
00:05:08,108 --> 00:05:22,234
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, yeah, usually, I would think, yeah, you calm down your breath, you make the breaths lighter or slower, and then that will affect your body, that's gonna affect your muscles, whatever.

57
00:05:22,274 --> 00:05:22,754
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

58
00:05:22,874 --> 00:05:25,035
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, so you try to mess around in a particular

59
00:05:25,539 --> 00:05:27,862
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, chasing this, there might result in that.

60
00:05:28,182 --> 00:05:40,017
[SPEAKER_01]: But basically, sometimes people say, yeah, but it helps me or... And the only reason it helps is because at the time, that particular thing they're trying to come down, stands for the experience as a body as a whole.

61
00:05:40,818 --> 00:05:53,625
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you're so concerned over, you know, I don't know, some particular aspect of your body that's worrying you, or that is extremely not calm, or whatever else, then, yeah, calming that down would stand for calming down the whole body.

62
00:05:53,645 --> 00:06:00,849
[SPEAKER_01]: Because basically, at that time, that particular aspect of the body stands for that which body is, in your experience, as a whole.

63
00:06:03,050 --> 00:06:07,092
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's why people who do have that view and do that, they can't do it at will.

64
00:06:07,862 --> 00:06:14,009
[SPEAKER_01]: They can only do it when it happens that the particular aspect they're focusing on stands for the body as a whole.

65
00:06:14,209 --> 00:06:15,591
[SPEAKER_01]: But that won't always be the case.

66
00:06:16,452 --> 00:06:26,403
[SPEAKER_01]: Sometimes there won't be any particular aspect and then it will be just kind of generic collection of particular aspects that stand for the single phenomenon of the body in this experience as a whole.

67
00:06:35,537 --> 00:06:55,603
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if the person wants to practice calming down the body, as a meditation, basically, well, initially, the most important thing would be to not allow himself, herself, to descend onto, like, particular aspects of the body.

68
00:06:55,703 --> 00:06:58,544
[SPEAKER_01]: Just stay with the mental thought of

69
00:06:59,550 --> 00:07:00,350
[SPEAKER_01]: Counting down the body.

70
00:07:01,091 --> 00:07:04,933
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, again, that's all it... it doesn't... you don't need more than that.

71
00:07:05,513 --> 00:07:07,674
[SPEAKER_01]: Counting down the body results in calmness.

72
00:07:07,854 --> 00:07:09,775
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, obviously, the mind needs more, wants more.

73
00:07:10,295 --> 00:07:11,636
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't need more, but wants more.

74
00:07:11,656 --> 00:07:12,516
[SPEAKER_01]: Wants to say words.

75
00:07:12,677 --> 00:07:13,437
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, exactly.

76
00:07:13,457 --> 00:07:17,219
[SPEAKER_01]: Wants to designate it in the in the terms that it's used to.

77
00:07:17,239 --> 00:07:19,040
[SPEAKER_01]: And those terms are not subtle enough.

78
00:07:19,898 --> 00:07:24,182
[SPEAKER_01]: to designate the phenomenon of counting down the body as a phenomenon, as a general phenomenon.

79
00:07:24,222 --> 00:07:25,143
[SPEAKER_01]: They're just not subtle enough.

80
00:07:25,623 --> 00:07:43,739
[SPEAKER_01]: So, as I said before, instead of then coursing up, so to speak, how the body feels and trying to pin it down and go into sensations or whatever else, you should actually define those designations you're trying to designate that gen... general feeling... sorry, general phenomenon of the body as a whole.

81
00:07:46,853 --> 00:07:54,596
[SPEAKER_01]: It begins to sound abstract, but that's, you know, whenever you read, like in the suttas, when the Buddha was speaking about meditation and perception of light or this, yeah, people lose it.

82
00:07:54,836 --> 00:07:56,516
[SPEAKER_01]: They can't... it's hard to understand.

83
00:08:00,238 --> 00:08:06,320
[SPEAKER_01]: But in this particular instance, basically, all you have to do is just remember that you have no choice but have your body right there and then.

84
00:08:07,961 --> 00:08:10,842
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, okay, learn how to relax without ending up

85
00:08:13,698 --> 00:08:17,660
[SPEAKER_01]: Thinking particular things, particular aspects about the body.

86
00:08:18,380 --> 00:08:20,241
[SPEAKER_01]: Just think about calm down.

87
00:08:20,421 --> 00:08:21,741
[SPEAKER_01]: What does that mean, as a phenomenon?

88
00:08:21,781 --> 00:08:22,582
[SPEAKER_01]: Calming down.

89
00:08:23,322 --> 00:08:24,062
[SPEAKER_01]: How is that done?

90
00:08:25,443 --> 00:08:27,584
[SPEAKER_01]: Not even specifically in regard to body.

91
00:08:27,824 --> 00:08:38,568
[SPEAKER_01]: Just I tell you, as an external person, as an other to you, I tell you, I utter a designation, calm down.

92
00:08:41,630 --> 00:08:42,510
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you do have

93
00:08:44,170 --> 00:08:48,292
[SPEAKER_01]: There's a degree of meaning in regard to that designation.

94
00:08:49,412 --> 00:08:59,297
[SPEAKER_01]: It might not correspond 100% to what I regard calming down is, but certainly enough that we are able to talk and discuss it and communicate to an extent.

95
00:09:01,458 --> 00:09:02,018
[SPEAKER_01]: But there's a thing.

96
00:09:02,098 --> 00:09:08,101
[SPEAKER_01]: For yourself, then, you can take up that designation, calm down, and see how far you can take it.

97
00:09:09,400 --> 00:09:17,566
[SPEAKER_01]: Without, you know, falling into some psychological explanations and things that kind of... No, no, it is this, or it might be that.

98
00:09:17,626 --> 00:09:18,046
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

99
00:09:18,086 --> 00:09:24,791
[SPEAKER_01]: Just for you, in your own terms, without resorting to anything external, an act of calming down.

100
00:09:26,492 --> 00:09:27,332
[SPEAKER_01]: Can you do it anytime?

101
00:09:27,412 --> 00:09:28,413
[SPEAKER_01]: Can you do it in regard to anything?

102
00:09:29,394 --> 00:09:30,314
[SPEAKER_01]: What is it, basically?

103
00:09:31,055 --> 00:09:31,755
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you discern?

104
00:09:31,835 --> 00:09:32,316
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you see?

105
00:09:34,477 --> 00:09:38,240
[SPEAKER_00]: Because I think it would be a mistake if I start imagining

106
00:09:39,163 --> 00:09:48,089
[SPEAKER_01]: That's again, that's like, basically, trying to induce the calmness, doing things that you think might actually result in it.

107
00:09:48,449 --> 00:09:52,191
[SPEAKER_01]: And they sometimes might, but not for the reasons you think they would.

108
00:09:53,853 --> 00:10:06,400
[SPEAKER_00]: But an image of, say, an image might arise in the mind due to the fact that I have the intention to calm, like I might have a...

109
00:10:08,752 --> 00:10:17,458
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, because your concern is not in regard to that image, but it's in regard to intention, to come, then those images become secondary.

110
00:10:25,865 --> 00:10:32,229
[SPEAKER_00]: So, things like, you're just talking about the itch or, let's say, there's like anger.

111
00:10:33,830 --> 00:10:35,792
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, you overcome with anger.

112
00:10:37,375 --> 00:10:40,157
[SPEAKER_00]: And your body is already in a state of shaking or whatever.

113
00:10:40,457 --> 00:10:44,620
[SPEAKER_00]: Not shouting, but just this anger.

114
00:10:44,660 --> 00:10:47,401
[SPEAKER_00]: So, intention to non-anger.

115
00:10:48,382 --> 00:10:51,844
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to have... you need to establish that.

116
00:10:52,084 --> 00:10:52,705
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's quite...

117
00:10:54,678 --> 00:10:56,180
[SPEAKER_00]: It's quite difficult at that stage.

118
00:10:56,220 --> 00:10:57,461
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean... Well, it is.

119
00:10:57,681 --> 00:10:58,021
[SPEAKER_01]: It is.

120
00:10:58,222 --> 00:11:12,655
[SPEAKER_01]: Basically, the only reason you were able to... well, the only reason one was able to get so angry in a state of, you know, being shaken by anger and so on, is because the intention of non-anger hasn't really been developed.

121
00:11:12,675 --> 00:11:14,497
[SPEAKER_01]: So, at that time, all you need to do is just greet your

122
00:11:18,052 --> 00:11:19,092
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the intention.

123
00:11:19,353 --> 00:11:29,357
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, when you're not that angry, practice the intention of non-anger, and practice the intention of dispelling the, you know, kind of looming anger, or looming bad mood, and so on.

124
00:11:29,377 --> 00:11:32,798
[SPEAKER_01]: Basically, seeing it at its very early stages, so to speak, right?

125
00:11:32,818 --> 00:11:33,479
[SPEAKER_01]: Discerning it.

126
00:11:34,499 --> 00:11:35,720
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, training in regards to that.

127
00:11:35,780 --> 00:11:39,541
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, it will never come to that fully-blown anger, where you're like... you don't know what you're doing.

128
00:11:40,622 --> 00:11:41,362
[SPEAKER_01]: The resistance.

129
00:11:42,903 --> 00:11:43,143
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

130
00:11:43,203 --> 00:11:47,665
[SPEAKER_01]: It starts with resistance, starts with aversion, and then you just...

131
00:11:48,770 --> 00:11:53,555
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't look at it, you ignore it, you turn a blind eye, you give in to it, because it's... oh, well, it's not...

132
00:11:53,575 --> 00:11:56,598
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm not as angry, so it's okay, and whatever else.

133
00:11:56,618 --> 00:12:02,143
[SPEAKER_01]: But then it ends up being accumulated, and then eventually you get very angry over nothing, over some silly things.

134
00:12:02,163 --> 00:12:07,889
[SPEAKER_01]: They just become kind of an excuse to let the whole thing out.

135
00:12:10,705 --> 00:12:12,047
[SPEAKER_01]: You're always aware, by the way.

136
00:12:12,367 --> 00:12:16,312
[SPEAKER_01]: People can never be like, completely, oh, yeah, I was so angry.

137
00:12:16,332 --> 00:12:27,466
[SPEAKER_01]: You always know what you're doing, because anger, or the same with lust, you know, as powerful as they can get, they're always rooted in bad faith, which means you always know what you're doing, but you're covering it up.

138
00:12:29,799 --> 00:12:32,221
[SPEAKER_00]: You're just not recognizing that you are.

139
00:12:32,441 --> 00:12:42,588
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you refuse, basically, to just... you even refuse to... yeah, you refuse to look at the fact that you know what you're doing, and then you just let that built-up anger or built-up lust go.

140
00:12:43,428 --> 00:12:44,269
[SPEAKER_00]: You just go.

141
00:12:45,029 --> 00:12:45,510
[SPEAKER_00]: Go with it.

142
00:12:46,751 --> 00:12:52,254
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, yeah, it's mindfulness, in a way, like knowing that you are aware of something.

143
00:12:52,274 --> 00:12:52,514
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

144
00:12:52,835 --> 00:12:54,996
[SPEAKER_01]: Admitting to yourself, yeah.

145
00:12:55,036 --> 00:12:55,997
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what mindfulness is.

146
00:12:57,168 --> 00:12:58,949
[SPEAKER_01]: Feeds authentic attitude.

147
00:12:59,510 --> 00:13:03,152
[SPEAKER_01]: Attitude of not... it's basically attitude of non-self-deception.

148
00:13:17,281 --> 00:13:20,403
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, another thing, maybe, is probably useful to mention.

149
00:13:21,390 --> 00:13:33,185
[SPEAKER_01]: Back onto meditation, if you just practice the intention of calming down the body, in general, or relaxing, or whatever you want to call it, it's important to know that then, yeah, you might start feeling your body differently.

150
00:13:35,027 --> 00:13:38,431
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, you know, you've been you've been cultivated the intention of calming down your body, you might just

151
00:13:39,501 --> 00:13:46,703
[SPEAKER_01]: You start feeling whatever, light or or or just, well, it's like, oh, I don't feel my body anymore.

152
00:13:47,383 --> 00:13:48,003
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's the thing.

153
00:13:48,304 --> 00:13:52,025
[SPEAKER_01]: That's all relative to your day-to-day life, how you're used to feeling your body.

154
00:13:52,065 --> 00:13:59,827
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not actually any accomplishment in itself, to, like, feel your body, I don't know, like light, cloud or whatever else.

155
00:14:00,087 --> 00:14:03,528
[SPEAKER_01]: It's all a novelty, just in regard to how you're used to feeling your body.

156
00:14:04,870 --> 00:14:13,234
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's all fine, as long as you don't then become sort of too concerned, too obsessed, too keen on having your body felt like that all the time.

157
00:14:13,754 --> 00:14:20,137
[SPEAKER_01]: That's again falling down onto particular and forgetting that it was a bad intention to just calm down the body.

158
00:14:20,637 --> 00:14:25,979
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, what that intention results in, it's all secondary and you do not care about that.

159
00:14:26,500 --> 00:14:29,441
[SPEAKER_01]: So, sometimes you can be very calm and your body will feel very normal.

160
00:14:29,801 --> 00:14:31,322
[SPEAKER_01]: You won't feel any like, you know,

161
00:14:32,663 --> 00:14:33,483
[SPEAKER_01]: Whatever.

162
00:14:33,603 --> 00:14:34,383
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, tingling.

163
00:14:34,623 --> 00:14:35,424
[SPEAKER_01]: Tingling sensation.

164
00:14:35,484 --> 00:14:37,624
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't know, but basically how people...

165
00:14:37,644 --> 00:14:40,204
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't know how people would describe it, but you know what I mean.

166
00:14:40,284 --> 00:14:51,166
[SPEAKER_01]: When you relax your body and then... Well, you might be struggling for words to describe it, because it becomes different to how you used to, kind of, perceive your body in your day-to-day life.

167
00:14:51,887 --> 00:14:57,948
[SPEAKER_01]: But, no matter how ambiguously you might be perceiving your body at the time, as a result of that calming down, that's irrelevant.

168
00:14:58,068 --> 00:14:59,008
[SPEAKER_01]: That was never the goal.

169
00:14:59,917 --> 00:15:06,722
[SPEAKER_01]: The goal is not to actually feel and perceive these certain ways the body can be felt or perceived.

170
00:15:08,264 --> 00:15:17,851
[SPEAKER_01]: It's about recognizing, discerning and cultivating intention to calm down the phenomenon of body, to calm down the nature of body.

171
00:15:19,852 --> 00:15:29,220
[SPEAKER_00]: You see, when you're doing this, practicing these kinds of intentions, and then seeing

172
00:15:31,709 --> 00:15:37,732
[SPEAKER_00]: See, noticing the result of these intentions, so far, an actual calm body, whatever particular thing's happening.

173
00:15:38,593 --> 00:15:38,793
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

174
00:15:40,394 --> 00:15:41,214
[SPEAKER_01]: Can get a bit green.

175
00:15:41,935 --> 00:15:44,456
[SPEAKER_01]: Well... No, well... Yeah, I mean...

176
00:15:45,076 --> 00:15:47,218
[SPEAKER_00]: But I always start to think about...

177
00:15:47,258 --> 00:15:49,759
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I have to think about the principle that's happening.

178
00:15:50,099 --> 00:15:50,359
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

179
00:15:50,399 --> 00:15:51,460
[SPEAKER_00]: That just determines this.

180
00:15:52,200 --> 00:15:55,562
[SPEAKER_00]: And without this, this wouldn't... Yeah.

181
00:15:55,582 --> 00:15:57,924
[SPEAKER_00]: ...be hap... You know, the other particular thing's going to be hap...

182
00:15:58,004 --> 00:15:58,644
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just...

183
00:15:58,664 --> 00:16:00,125
[SPEAKER_01]: It's basically just another step.

184
00:16:01,741 --> 00:16:03,182
[SPEAKER_01]: In a course of developing your mind.

185
00:16:03,842 --> 00:16:08,285
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you start developing your mind in regard to recognize the intention to calm down.

186
00:16:09,026 --> 00:16:10,107
[SPEAKER_01]: Calming down the body.

187
00:16:10,767 --> 00:16:18,092
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, the pleasant results as a result of that... Now, you have to train your mind in regard to them, because now they're pulling you a bit too much.

188
00:16:18,733 --> 00:16:21,935
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, you want to always have that calm body and never... No.

189
00:16:22,435 --> 00:16:24,156
[SPEAKER_01]: That calm body wasn't yours to begin with.

190
00:16:25,237 --> 00:16:26,378
[SPEAKER_01]: The intention was.

191
00:16:26,558 --> 00:16:28,039
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, even the intention wasn't yours.

192
00:16:28,399 --> 00:16:28,560
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay.

193
00:16:28,580 --> 00:16:30,421
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that intention would be...

194
00:16:32,725 --> 00:16:33,426
[SPEAKER_01]: Indiscernible?

195
00:16:35,328 --> 00:16:35,948
[SPEAKER_00]: Indiscernible.

196
00:16:36,489 --> 00:16:39,251
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't know if that is a word, but it would be impossible to discern.

197
00:16:40,512 --> 00:16:44,796
[SPEAKER_01]: That intention would be impossible to discern if that body was not already there.

198
00:16:44,856 --> 00:16:46,838
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that body pertains...

199
00:16:47,699 --> 00:16:50,601
[SPEAKER_01]: Sorry, because that intention pertains to that body.

200
00:16:51,182 --> 00:16:58,128
[SPEAKER_01]: Every intention is mental, but because it pertains to the body, in its nature, in regards to the body, it's a bodily intention.

201
00:17:01,258 --> 00:17:03,239
[SPEAKER_01]: But every intention manifests on the level of the mind.

202
00:17:04,720 --> 00:17:05,860
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's a bodily intention.

203
00:17:08,262 --> 00:17:10,543
[SPEAKER_01]: So the body has a possibility to be calm.

204
00:17:11,283 --> 00:17:17,686
[SPEAKER_01]: So all you're doing, you're attending possibility of calm in regard to body, and that's a bodily intention of calming down.

205
00:17:19,327 --> 00:17:24,810
[SPEAKER_01]: You are... there is no subject there that it's actually producing that intention towards this external body.

206
00:17:26,068 --> 00:17:34,110
[SPEAKER_01]: Or rather, that which is the subject producing the intention in regards to the external body, that subject also belongs to that external body through that intention.

207
00:17:34,650 --> 00:17:35,850
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, it's not really a subject.

208
00:17:38,950 --> 00:17:43,471
[SPEAKER_00]: Because there's the the calmness of the body, the intention to calm.

209
00:17:44,531 --> 00:17:46,392
[SPEAKER_01]: It's all in regards to the body and the nature of the world.

210
00:17:48,532 --> 00:17:52,473
[SPEAKER_01]: And that subject that inevitably appears for kutujana in regards to the body,

211
00:17:53,715 --> 00:17:54,095
[SPEAKER_01]: It's there.

212
00:17:54,195 --> 00:17:57,478
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like a counterpart, a negative sort of phenomenon regarding the whole thing.

213
00:17:57,838 --> 00:18:03,362
[SPEAKER_01]: And you don't have to worry about it in terms of trying to abolish it or remove it.

214
00:18:03,442 --> 00:18:04,142
[SPEAKER_01]: That's impossible.

215
00:18:04,563 --> 00:18:07,965
[SPEAKER_01]: You actually have to acknowledge it and then stop regarding it as yours.

216
00:18:08,385 --> 00:18:08,826
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's it.

217
00:18:09,066 --> 00:18:09,726
[SPEAKER_01]: That's all you take.

218
00:18:10,287 --> 00:18:14,690
[SPEAKER_01]: And by not regarding it as yours, that subject will cease to be the center of your experience.

219
00:18:16,494 --> 00:18:25,861
[SPEAKER_01]: It just becomes an individual arising and ceasing in the same way pañca-khandhā arises and ceases until they completely break apart in parinibbāna.

220
00:18:25,881 --> 00:18:26,282
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

221
00:18:26,982 --> 00:18:29,644
[SPEAKER_00]: What about awareness that's there?

222
00:18:29,764 --> 00:18:42,755
[SPEAKER_00]: There's attention being paid to certain things, like recognizing the... Well, the awareness in itself, you can't discern that.

223
00:18:42,775 --> 00:18:44,056
[SPEAKER_01]: There's no such thing as pure awareness.

224
00:18:45,802 --> 00:18:56,869
[SPEAKER_01]: See, like, the thing is, you can say, oh, pure awareness, but you fail to see that your designation, the very words you used as pure awareness, stand for the matter in regard to which you're aware.

225
00:18:57,790 --> 00:19:06,196
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, there is no pure awareness as such, because if there were, well, you wouldn't be able to utter it, you wouldn't be able to discern it, and in that way, it means it can't be.

226
00:19:06,516 --> 00:19:10,559
[SPEAKER_00]: I would say there is the noticing of that.

227
00:19:10,799 --> 00:19:11,059
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

228
00:19:12,200 --> 00:19:13,941
[SPEAKER_00]: Also present with

229
00:19:15,506 --> 00:19:16,788
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure.

230
00:19:17,009 --> 00:19:18,671
[SPEAKER_01]: You can discern that.

231
00:19:21,014 --> 00:19:26,182
[SPEAKER_01]: But you can't, in the same sense that Buddha said, you can't separate feelings, perception and intention.

232
00:19:26,518 --> 00:19:28,780
[SPEAKER_01]: They're conjoined, they're not disjoined, it all comes one.

233
00:19:29,460 --> 00:19:30,401
[SPEAKER_01]: An examiner...

234
00:19:30,441 --> 00:19:32,923
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's all I'm pointing out, basically.

235
00:19:33,083 --> 00:19:42,090
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, you can say there's awareness in regard to it and there is noticing of it, but just make sure you don't mystify it.

236
00:19:42,270 --> 00:19:49,856
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't become an entity in itself of pure noticing, pure awareness, pristine awareness, or all sorts of terms you might end up using.

237
00:19:50,196 --> 00:19:52,297
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to see it in the context.

238
00:19:52,337 --> 00:19:53,057
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, no.

239
00:19:53,077 --> 00:19:55,238
[SPEAKER_01]: You just have to discern, because it's always in the context.

240
00:19:55,278 --> 00:19:56,198
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't have to see it.

241
00:19:56,439 --> 00:19:57,639
[SPEAKER_01]: It is always in the context.

242
00:19:57,739 --> 00:19:57,939
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

243
00:19:58,159 --> 00:20:00,680
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you just have to actually find it in context.

244
00:20:01,200 --> 00:20:02,701
[SPEAKER_01]: In the context that it has arisen.

245
00:20:02,981 --> 00:20:03,681
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the thing.

246
00:20:04,222 --> 00:20:09,824
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you might recognize it in the context once or twice, and then you develop a view that this thing is always in the context.

247
00:20:10,564 --> 00:20:14,047
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you put the context over the actually already arisen context.

248
00:20:14,727 --> 00:20:18,930
[SPEAKER_01]: And even if that is the same context, the fact that you are putting it over means you're assuming it.

249
00:20:19,271 --> 00:20:20,431
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the wrong thing to do.

250
00:20:20,832 --> 00:20:22,153
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if you're assuming it rightly.

251
00:20:24,254 --> 00:20:28,918
[SPEAKER_01]: So, eventually, you want to stop assuming any context and just be discerning the arisen context.

252
00:20:37,369 --> 00:20:46,517
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, by knowing that that context is always there, you know, ah, it's just a question of time of me discerning it, if that's how you look at it.

253
00:20:48,578 --> 00:20:53,643
[SPEAKER_01]: And you can discern it by learning how to, well, see it, so to speak.

254
00:20:53,663 --> 00:20:59,448
[SPEAKER_01]: Not by writing it with your own terms, but actually reading it

255
00:21:00,630 --> 00:21:01,431
[SPEAKER_01]: The way it has arisen.

256
00:21:01,951 --> 00:21:06,235
[SPEAKER_01]: But in order to read it, you know, you have to know how, you know, to know how it's written, so to speak.

257
00:21:06,275 --> 00:21:07,537
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to learn that language.

258
00:21:07,557 --> 00:21:09,378
[SPEAKER_01]: And that language is nothing other than what I said before.

259
00:21:09,398 --> 00:21:23,392
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just basically training your mind in, well, in becoming subtler than the designation that you used to, well, used to have, used to entertain in regard to these things that will pertain to the mind.

260
00:21:23,432 --> 00:21:24,653
[SPEAKER_01]: So, for example, many people

261
00:21:26,394 --> 00:21:28,556
[SPEAKER_01]: wouldn't say that their thoughts exist.

262
00:21:29,357 --> 00:21:30,638
[SPEAKER_01]: Because for them, they're used to.

263
00:21:30,778 --> 00:21:37,403
[SPEAKER_01]: For them, the measure of existence is what they're so used to, so entrenched in, that they get through senses.

264
00:21:38,544 --> 00:21:39,966
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, their thoughts are just these

265
00:21:40,948 --> 00:21:43,551
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I mean, but they fail to see that it's a little bit of a contradiction.

266
00:21:43,991 --> 00:21:49,757
[SPEAKER_01]: Because just by saying, oh, my thoughts don't really exist, you were designating that very thing, that then you deny to be existing.

267
00:21:50,117 --> 00:21:50,337
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

268
00:21:50,397 --> 00:22:03,110
[SPEAKER_01]: Because for you, bhāva, existence, has been, like, quite, sort of, charged up, brought up, far out, whereby now pertains to the just senses and the palpable aspects of your five senses.

269
00:22:04,317 --> 00:22:07,079
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how bhāva grows, bhāva accumulates.

270
00:22:07,139 --> 00:22:09,100
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not a static thing, so to speak.

271
00:22:09,140 --> 00:22:09,940
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, it's nature.

272
00:22:10,040 --> 00:22:10,621
[SPEAKER_01]: Bhāva-bhāva.

273
00:22:10,701 --> 00:22:11,561
[SPEAKER_01]: Existence-existence.

274
00:22:11,601 --> 00:22:14,143
[SPEAKER_01]: But for some people, things can exist more.

275
00:22:15,083 --> 00:22:21,007
[SPEAKER_01]: If they have more significance, if they have more weight, if there is more lust and aversion and delusion in regard to it, they proliferate.

276
00:22:22,408 --> 00:22:23,128
[SPEAKER_01]: They accumulate.

277
00:22:23,829 --> 00:22:24,649
[SPEAKER_01]: They get heavier.

278
00:22:27,951 --> 00:22:28,852
[SPEAKER_00]: It's hard to stop you.

279
00:22:29,497 --> 00:22:35,141
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you stop by recognizing that, oh, hold on, if my thoughts don't exist, how can I even think about it?

280
00:22:36,061 --> 00:22:42,365
[SPEAKER_01]: So, they exist, just much subtler than things that you might grab with your hand or see with your eyes.

281
00:22:42,405 --> 00:22:44,506
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you start recognizing what's in common then?

282
00:22:44,687 --> 00:22:50,590
[SPEAKER_01]: What is in common to things that I can see with my eyes, things I can grab with my hand, and things I can think about?

283
00:22:51,911 --> 00:22:56,294
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it's... what is in common is that they actually, all of them, are thought of.

284
00:22:56,994 --> 00:22:58,095
[SPEAKER_01]: They're all mental images.

285
00:22:58,587 --> 00:22:59,327
[SPEAKER_01]: They're all dhammas.

286
00:23:01,208 --> 00:23:02,009
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, there you go.

287
00:23:02,589 --> 00:23:05,171
[SPEAKER_01]: You already got the nature of things.

288
00:23:05,191 --> 00:23:08,733
[SPEAKER_01]: The nature of things is to appear as an image, to arise on its own.

289
00:23:09,213 --> 00:23:11,454
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you recognize, yeah, that is what comes first.

290
00:23:11,834 --> 00:23:13,615
[SPEAKER_01]: Not the fact that I'm holding it in my hand.

291
00:23:14,496 --> 00:23:22,961
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm only able to hold this thing in my hand, is because there was already a mental image of a hand, things to be held, a sense, a sense, sense object.

292
00:23:23,101 --> 00:23:24,461
[SPEAKER_01]: All of that was already manifested.

293
00:23:24,501 --> 00:23:26,783
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the senses will not operate.

294
00:23:27,756 --> 00:23:33,538
[SPEAKER_01]: If the mind is not, you know, the reign, sort of, if the mind is not in control of them.

295
00:23:34,278 --> 00:23:36,858
[SPEAKER_01]: Obviously, the mind is not in control, because people don't practice restraint.

296
00:23:37,539 --> 00:23:40,059
[SPEAKER_01]: But they do meet in mind, and mind has a final end.

297
00:23:40,119 --> 00:23:41,860
[SPEAKER_01]: Let's say, mind comes first.

298
00:23:48,281 --> 00:23:52,542
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then it just comes down to repetition of that recognition of what these dhammas are.

299
00:23:53,583 --> 00:23:54,263
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, this is...

300
00:23:55,127 --> 00:23:58,952
[SPEAKER_01]: An object, but again, the ego, you designate it as an object, which is a mental image.

301
00:24:00,094 --> 00:24:00,975
[SPEAKER_01]: That whole designation.

302
00:24:01,275 --> 00:24:05,841
[SPEAKER_01]: So, everything becomes recognized, because everything already is a mental image.

303
00:24:06,282 --> 00:24:09,426
[SPEAKER_01]: A mental image that pertains to senses, a mental image that pertains to a mental image.

304
00:24:09,746 --> 00:24:10,247
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the mind.

305
00:24:20,197 --> 00:24:28,983
[SPEAKER_01]: And the more you practice that, the more your mind withdraws from the threshold of being that was set far out in the in the sense world.

306
00:24:29,483 --> 00:24:31,564
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you recognize... it just... it just fades away.

307
00:24:32,245 --> 00:24:35,467
[SPEAKER_01]: Because now the mind recognizes more, greater nature of it.

308
00:24:43,432 --> 00:24:43,992
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, what about this?

309
00:24:46,093 --> 00:24:47,454
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, dharma is ceasing.

310
00:24:48,275 --> 00:24:48,695
[SPEAKER_01]: Ceasing.

311
00:24:49,374 --> 00:24:51,716
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that's when the mind ceases to be established upon anything.

312
00:24:53,657 --> 00:24:57,160
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, you do have things arising and ceasing, but they don't really exist.

313
00:24:57,660 --> 00:25:10,310
[SPEAKER_01]: Because existence, bhāva, is that significance, charged with that significance of mind, form, significance of lust, aversion or delusion.

314
00:25:10,430 --> 00:25:16,875
[SPEAKER_01]: So you remove that, and then you can say you just have mere arising and mere ceasing, but they don't exist.

315
00:25:19,577 --> 00:25:20,198
[SPEAKER_01]: Existence.

316
00:25:20,358 --> 00:25:21,818
[SPEAKER_01]: That's again...

317
00:25:21,858 --> 00:25:26,620
[SPEAKER_01]: I think, well, that's what I wrote about in that article, in that essay, Appearance is an Existence, for Putrajana.

318
00:25:27,701 --> 00:25:30,102
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, basically, let's say like this.

319
00:25:30,222 --> 00:25:34,764
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't have existence without appearance, but you can have appearance without existence.

320
00:25:36,925 --> 00:25:41,727
[SPEAKER_01]: But obviously, most of people who are far down

321
00:25:43,211 --> 00:25:46,754
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, at the end of existence, when they look back, all they see is existence.

322
00:25:46,914 --> 00:25:48,715
[SPEAKER_01]: So, for them, appearance, existence is the same.

323
00:25:49,335 --> 00:25:57,300
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you actually start walking up, reversing that thing, there will be a point where you went across, and then it goes, no, things appear, and they don't exist anymore.

324
00:25:59,482 --> 00:26:01,243
[SPEAKER_00]: It's kind of no weight, so to speak.

325
00:26:01,303 --> 00:26:02,023
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, exactly.

326
00:26:02,043 --> 00:26:03,764
[SPEAKER_00]: They're drained, dead.

327
00:26:05,165 --> 00:26:07,287
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, with a root cut off, like a palm stump.

328
00:26:08,007 --> 00:26:10,809
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the palm stump is still there, appearing, but it's dead.

329
00:26:11,582 --> 00:26:12,762
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not charged anymore.

330
00:26:12,802 --> 00:26:14,063
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not proliferating anymore.

331
00:26:15,243 --> 00:26:17,044
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it doesn't induce feelings anymore.

332
00:26:17,064 --> 00:26:19,445
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't... it cannot be the cause of suffering anymore.

333
00:26:19,945 --> 00:26:20,865
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just done.

334
00:26:21,766 --> 00:26:22,146
[SPEAKER_01]: Cut up.

335
00:26:23,566 --> 00:26:25,547
[SPEAKER_01]: And even that will break apart eventually.

336
00:26:25,567 --> 00:26:28,608
[SPEAKER_01]: For another time, even the appearance breaks apart when the ideas break apart.

337
00:26:29,328 --> 00:26:30,369
[SPEAKER_01]: And it doesn't... that's it.

338
00:26:31,189 --> 00:26:32,129
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't re-emerge.

339
00:26:32,630 --> 00:26:33,390
[SPEAKER_01]: For that very reason.

340
00:26:33,410 --> 00:26:37,511
[SPEAKER_01]: You re-emerged because of that charge in regard to appearance remaining.

341
00:26:38,532 --> 00:26:39,532
[SPEAKER_01]: Even at the time of death.

342
00:26:47,057 --> 00:26:52,761
[SPEAKER_01]: But you remove the significance of mind, the personality, the self.

343
00:26:52,801 --> 00:26:57,425
[SPEAKER_01]: All of those things come from that very same charge of existence.

344
00:26:58,666 --> 00:26:58,966
[SPEAKER_00]: Weight.

345
00:26:59,467 --> 00:27:00,007
[SPEAKER_01]: Weight to it.

346
00:27:03,029 --> 00:27:07,293
[SPEAKER_01]: And again, you can actually, you can arrive at that by just what I said.

347
00:27:08,272 --> 00:27:09,833
[SPEAKER_01]: You can say, sure, everything exists.

348
00:27:10,613 --> 00:27:13,815
[SPEAKER_01]: Means everything... well, everything affects you in a way.

349
00:27:14,015 --> 00:27:16,316
[SPEAKER_01]: Everything has a certain significance to you.

350
00:27:16,736 --> 00:27:16,956
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

351
00:27:16,996 --> 00:27:18,517
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you take yourself to be.

352
00:27:18,537 --> 00:27:18,897
[SPEAKER_01]: You are.

353
00:27:19,337 --> 00:27:19,637
[SPEAKER_01]: I am.

354
00:27:23,839 --> 00:27:28,261
[SPEAKER_01]: So, all of that existence, what does it have in common then?

355
00:27:29,302 --> 00:27:35,505
[SPEAKER_01]: Existence of the sense objects, of the senses, of the sense pleasures, existence of the thoughts, abstract thoughts, imaginary thoughts,

356
00:27:37,024 --> 00:27:41,665
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, what it has in common is, it appears as a mental image.

357
00:27:42,105 --> 00:27:47,706
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, yeah, that mental image is affected with a parasite that kind of makes you think it's for you.

358
00:27:48,206 --> 00:27:49,387
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, I must be.

359
00:27:51,007 --> 00:27:55,368
[SPEAKER_01]: But, obviously, you can undo that only once you first acknowledge it as a mental image.

360
00:27:56,748 --> 00:27:58,509
[SPEAKER_01]: And that mental image is not in your head.

361
00:27:58,949 --> 00:28:00,829
[SPEAKER_01]: That mental image is right there where the things are.

362
00:28:01,109 --> 00:28:05,430
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the distance, say, looking at the other side of the valley, on that hill over there,

363
00:28:06,649 --> 00:28:08,571
[SPEAKER_01]: Mental image is right there, there, out there.

364
00:28:09,011 --> 00:28:11,693
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, saying out there, that's another mental image.

365
00:28:11,713 --> 00:28:14,275
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the whole space, it's a mental image.

366
00:28:14,295 --> 00:28:15,276
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in the sense...

367
00:28:15,296 --> 00:28:21,261
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, it's probably not the best way to call a mental image, because for most of the people it would imply, ah, it's all in my head.

368
00:28:21,982 --> 00:28:25,645
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, it would be like a form of idealism, saying it's all in my... it's all in my mind.

369
00:28:25,785 --> 00:28:25,965
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

370
00:28:26,505 --> 00:28:29,548
[SPEAKER_01]: Your mind is not yours to begin with, which means your mind is out there with things.

371
00:28:31,049 --> 00:28:31,770
[SPEAKER_01]: And you are not there.

372
00:28:35,652 --> 00:28:41,177
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can just say an image, or you can just say a dhamma, or a thought.

373
00:28:41,217 --> 00:28:51,065
[SPEAKER_01]: But again, thought already has connotation for many people, as opposed to the senses, or this is the real world, and then this is the world of my thoughts.

374
00:28:51,145 --> 00:28:59,853
[SPEAKER_01]: But whether it's a thought, whether it's the, in verse of karma, real world, what they have in common, they have in common to appear as an image.

375
00:29:01,752 --> 00:29:02,512
[SPEAKER_01]: As a phenomenon.

376
00:29:03,072 --> 00:29:07,334
[SPEAKER_01]: But now, obviously, for Europeans saying phenomenon, ah, it's just philosophy.

377
00:29:08,854 --> 00:29:10,795
[SPEAKER_01]: That's just some weird philosophy we've done, you know.

378
00:29:11,155 --> 00:29:11,915
[SPEAKER_01]: There's nothing to it.

379
00:29:12,795 --> 00:29:24,198
[SPEAKER_01]: Call it whatever you want, but phenomenon would be the most... well, it would be the most accurate, actually, designation for it to appear.

380
00:29:25,219 --> 00:29:26,199
[SPEAKER_01]: As a nature to appear.

381
00:29:27,859 --> 00:29:28,220
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

382
00:29:28,480 --> 00:29:29,560
[SPEAKER_01]: Everything has a nature to appear.

383
00:29:31,699 --> 00:29:38,164
[SPEAKER_01]: Even the most abstract, ambiguous, impossible-to-define thoughts, they appear as such.

384
00:29:41,667 --> 00:29:47,912
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, when you start discerning the appearance within existence, existence would become redundant, eventually.

385
00:29:50,214 --> 00:29:52,076
[SPEAKER_00]: Everything becomes equal, in that sense.

386
00:29:52,977 --> 00:29:54,858
[SPEAKER_01]: In an emotional sense, yes.

387
00:29:54,878 --> 00:29:55,939
[SPEAKER_01]: Nothing is wiped out.

388
00:29:56,019 --> 00:29:57,760
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's why arahant is always at peace.

389
00:29:59,162 --> 00:29:59,642
[SPEAKER_01]: Immovable.

390
00:30:00,934 --> 00:30:01,754
[SPEAKER_01]: There's no like...

391
00:30:01,975 --> 00:30:07,838
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, there are inherent significances, you know, that still stays.

392
00:30:08,098 --> 00:30:17,584
[SPEAKER_01]: So, like, you wouldn't start using, you know, a cup to sweet the leaves, because that's not significance of the cup.

393
00:30:18,484 --> 00:30:30,468
[SPEAKER_01]: But none of these significances would be implying... would... well, sorry, would contain any more any emotional significance of my cup, my dear cup, or my lovely broom.

394
00:30:30,488 --> 00:30:32,268
[SPEAKER_01]: I really like this broom, it's really practical or...

395
00:30:33,148 --> 00:30:35,029
[SPEAKER_01]: It won't be for you, it won't be yours.

396
00:30:35,349 --> 00:30:38,030
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it would be a cup for drinking, broom for sweeping.

397
00:30:39,139 --> 00:30:46,202
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, if you want to mess about, you can try sweeping leaves with a cup, but it would be ridiculous and it wouldn't work.

398
00:30:46,462 --> 00:30:50,183
[SPEAKER_01]: But then again, it would be an intention to try something ridiculous.

399
00:30:50,403 --> 00:30:51,724
[SPEAKER_01]: But even that wouldn't be for you.

400
00:30:53,064 --> 00:30:56,045
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in that sense, you would not be affected by any of it.

401
00:30:57,646 --> 00:31:02,688
[SPEAKER_01]: And you certainly wouldn't be able to feel any suffering or pressure of any feeling in regard to it.

402
00:31:05,579 --> 00:31:08,141
[SPEAKER_01]: But it doesn't mean your mind becomes blank or something.

403
00:31:08,181 --> 00:31:09,302
[SPEAKER_01]: No, everything stays the same.

404
00:31:10,283 --> 00:31:12,766
[SPEAKER_01]: You just don't feel anything in regard to it anymore.

405
00:31:13,506 --> 00:31:18,531
[SPEAKER_01]: But it doesn't mean that you negate or destroy or ignore significance of things.

406
00:31:18,771 --> 00:31:23,095
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why arahants were not recognized by ordinary people.

407
00:31:23,135 --> 00:31:23,615
[SPEAKER_01]: External.

408
00:31:24,316 --> 00:31:27,859
[SPEAKER_01]: Sometimes they look worse, their behavior or things they would say.

409
00:31:34,998 --> 00:31:47,844
[SPEAKER_00]: So, since we're talking about existence, bhārata-bhāva, and it's, eh... but you're just saying, eh... assumption determines bhārata-bhāva.

410
00:31:47,864 --> 00:31:48,044
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

411
00:31:50,005 --> 00:31:51,786
[SPEAKER_00]: That's, that's the ultimate.

412
00:31:53,287 --> 00:32:00,130
[SPEAKER_01]: And not... Yeah, based on, on the, on the extent of your assumption, that's how bhārata-bhāva will be established.

413
00:32:02,832 --> 00:32:03,732
[SPEAKER_00]: The assumption being,

414
00:32:05,528 --> 00:32:06,128
[SPEAKER_01]: In your assumption?

415
00:32:06,428 --> 00:32:06,749
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

416
00:32:07,009 --> 00:32:11,191
[SPEAKER_00]: This is... not seeing it as... as it appears, first of all.

417
00:32:12,031 --> 00:32:13,112
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, yeah, yeah, yeah.

418
00:32:13,292 --> 00:32:24,597
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, assumption is basically... you take it to be something, without actually knowing whether that is that... what you take it to be.

419
00:32:24,617 --> 00:32:27,498
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I said, like, assuming a context, what you said before.

420
00:32:29,219 --> 00:32:32,381
[SPEAKER_01]: There is a context of... of... of... when there is an experience.

421
00:32:33,241 --> 00:32:35,122
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, you can also assume the context.

422
00:32:36,720 --> 00:32:38,121
[SPEAKER_01]: Often there is an experience.

423
00:32:39,882 --> 00:32:44,044
[SPEAKER_01]: And for you at the time, because you assume it, it is the real context.

424
00:32:44,904 --> 00:32:48,106
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't correspond to the actual context of the arisen experience.

425
00:32:49,167 --> 00:32:53,249
[SPEAKER_01]: But because all you have is your assumption of that context, it is what it is.

426
00:32:53,389 --> 00:32:53,869
[SPEAKER_01]: It is that.

427
00:32:55,310 --> 00:33:00,532
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why once you undo upādāna, the assumption, you can't arise again.

428
00:33:02,774 --> 00:33:03,754
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you can only arise

429
00:33:07,139 --> 00:33:11,840
[SPEAKER_01]: When you don't really... when you take things at face value, when you don't really know if it is like that or not.

430
00:33:12,261 --> 00:33:15,021
[SPEAKER_01]: Once you know that it is like that or not, you can't then...

431
00:33:15,342 --> 00:33:17,042
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, now I'll assume that it is this.

432
00:33:20,823 --> 00:33:22,004
[SPEAKER_01]: It's basically an assumption.

433
00:33:22,164 --> 00:33:32,287
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like taking a stand in regard to anything, without actually knowing whether that stand is right or not.

434
00:33:35,357 --> 00:33:37,118
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, that goes to very subtle levels.

435
00:33:37,238 --> 00:33:39,240
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you assume an assumption, you've got to sense it.

436
00:33:39,620 --> 00:33:42,022
[SPEAKER_01]: And the fundamental assumption that things are for you.

437
00:33:43,203 --> 00:33:44,104
[SPEAKER_01]: That things are yours.

438
00:33:44,964 --> 00:33:46,926
[SPEAKER_01]: And you don't have to worry about trying to chase this.

439
00:33:46,946 --> 00:33:51,629
[SPEAKER_01]: Just start with that fundamental assumption and it will undo all of the other assumptions.

440
00:33:52,170 --> 00:33:54,191
[SPEAKER_01]: Assumptions of sensuality and views and everything else.

441
00:33:54,431 --> 00:33:55,952
[SPEAKER_01]: Assumption of me.

442
00:33:57,934 --> 00:33:58,594
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you don't...

443
00:33:58,674 --> 00:34:00,976
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, and you can actually ask that to anybody.

444
00:34:01,276 --> 00:34:02,397
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, so what is that you?

445
00:34:02,577 --> 00:34:03,458
[SPEAKER_01]: Where are you?

446
00:34:03,738 --> 00:34:04,078
[SPEAKER_01]: Who are you?

447
00:34:04,319 --> 00:34:04,719
[SPEAKER_01]: What is it?

448
00:34:06,778 --> 00:34:07,298
[SPEAKER_01]: And you wouldn't...

449
00:34:07,378 --> 00:34:09,580
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, there is a very strong notion of me.

450
00:34:10,080 --> 00:34:18,745
[SPEAKER_01]: And that notion is real, because that assumption of for me has been never questioned, never challenged, never discerned.

451
00:34:19,245 --> 00:34:24,068
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, you did really develop a sense of me, based on constantly assuming that things are for you.

452
00:34:31,474 --> 00:34:41,936
[SPEAKER_01]: So, just stop assuming that things are for you, and then the assumption of me will fade, because you won't be feeling it anymore.

453
00:34:42,617 --> 00:34:48,058
[SPEAKER_00]: What about this, eh... you know, when people ask you, what is myself?

454
00:34:48,838 --> 00:34:54,519
[SPEAKER_00]: And it says, you know, why are questions about self and the world unanswerable?

455
00:34:55,120 --> 00:35:01,161
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, the question you just asked, why... because it implies that there is actually something that is yourself.

456
00:35:02,851 --> 00:35:04,432
[SPEAKER_01]: You say, what is my self?

457
00:35:04,452 --> 00:35:11,176
[SPEAKER_01]: It means, yeah, so we both agree that there is your self and now you're just asking me what it is.

458
00:35:11,196 --> 00:35:14,418
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's a wrong question because we don't agree that there is your self.

459
00:35:14,558 --> 00:35:15,979
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't agree.

460
00:35:15,999 --> 00:35:23,704
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you ask me that question, I'm not going to answer it in a way that would just sort of make you think you were right to ask it in the first place.

461
00:35:24,304 --> 00:35:28,427
[SPEAKER_01]: And you were not right to ask it in the first place because that question assumes the sense of self.

462
00:35:29,553 --> 00:35:30,373
[SPEAKER_01]: He doesn't question.

463
00:35:30,474 --> 00:35:30,854
[SPEAKER_01]: Hold on.

464
00:35:31,294 --> 00:35:33,475
[SPEAKER_01]: Is there a sense of self?

465
00:35:33,535 --> 00:35:35,176
[SPEAKER_01]: Am I wrong in thinking that I am?

466
00:35:35,236 --> 00:35:35,916
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't ask that.

467
00:35:35,937 --> 00:35:37,257
[SPEAKER_01]: You ask, what is myself?

468
00:35:37,297 --> 00:35:37,477
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

469
00:35:37,938 --> 00:35:40,199
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you already take for granted that it's yourself.

470
00:35:40,459 --> 00:35:41,980
[SPEAKER_01]: So, now you just need to know what it is.

471
00:35:42,760 --> 00:35:46,342
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is kind of funny, because it's like you really know what it is.

472
00:35:46,762 --> 00:35:47,683
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, exactly.

473
00:35:47,723 --> 00:35:48,023
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

474
00:35:48,043 --> 00:35:49,544
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the Buddha would be silent.

475
00:35:50,284 --> 00:35:57,608
[SPEAKER_01]: Or reply in a, well, seemingly unrelated manner, saying, describing patitsaṁpāda, describing the

476
00:35:59,459 --> 00:36:01,260
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, independent origination.

477
00:36:14,809 --> 00:36:24,175
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you were to see that, again, things arise on their own, they manifest, the nature is to be manifest, and you're not saying that.

478
00:36:26,031 --> 00:36:30,072
[SPEAKER_01]: Anything, be it your senses, your thoughts, you already find there all you can do.

479
00:36:30,372 --> 00:36:34,553
[SPEAKER_01]: You can intend on top of it, you can, you know, you can pursue it, you can build your whole life.

480
00:36:35,293 --> 00:36:41,074
[SPEAKER_01]: But, fundamentally, your senses and things in regard to your senses, they're just there.

481
00:36:41,554 --> 00:36:42,394
[SPEAKER_01]: You just use them.

482
00:36:43,335 --> 00:36:50,196
[SPEAKER_01]: And you certainly have no access to, nor even a view into, where they came from.

483
00:36:51,141 --> 00:36:56,884
[SPEAKER_01]: Or even just asking where, even the assumption that they came from somewhere, doesn't even... even that doesn't apply to them.

484
00:36:57,604 --> 00:37:00,005
[SPEAKER_01]: They're just given, and then they go.

485
00:37:01,146 --> 00:37:02,266
[SPEAKER_01]: When you die, basically.

486
00:37:02,686 --> 00:37:05,068
[SPEAKER_01]: So, just recognizing that, that they're inaccessible.

487
00:37:05,808 --> 00:37:07,949
[SPEAKER_01]: All you can do is, like, secondarily use them.

488
00:37:09,812 --> 00:37:13,194
[SPEAKER_01]: Means they can't really be yours, because if it's yours, you would have it.

489
00:37:13,735 --> 00:37:18,298
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why it's often in the city like a borrowed or a gold or jewelry.

490
00:37:18,338 --> 00:37:19,019
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not yours.

491
00:37:19,039 --> 00:37:20,460
[SPEAKER_01]: It's gonna be, and you know.

492
00:37:21,040 --> 00:37:22,441
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, everybody knows they'll die.

493
00:37:22,501 --> 00:37:24,382
[SPEAKER_01]: Everybody knows the senses will fall apart.

494
00:37:26,364 --> 00:37:28,725
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, you still go ahead, use it as if it belongs to you.

495
00:37:29,766 --> 00:37:35,070
[SPEAKER_01]: So, all you have to do is remove that sense of belonging in regard to things that you're using.

496
00:37:36,251 --> 00:37:37,652
[SPEAKER_01]: And what are the things that you're using?

497
00:37:37,952 --> 00:37:39,413
[SPEAKER_01]: Your eyes, your nose, your ears.

498
00:37:40,713 --> 00:37:43,355
[SPEAKER_01]: Your mind, basically, all of the senses and your mind...

499
00:37:44,015 --> 00:37:54,162
[SPEAKER_01]: There was in that sutra, when they... when the Buddha said, it's because people take that which belongs to Mara, Mara does what he wants with them, on account of it.

500
00:37:54,942 --> 00:37:56,523
[SPEAKER_01]: What are the things that belong to Mara?

501
00:37:56,923 --> 00:37:59,665
[SPEAKER_01]: It's eyes and forms, ears and sounds.

502
00:37:59,925 --> 00:38:02,707
[SPEAKER_01]: Basically, it's pretty much nothing else in between.

503
00:38:02,747 --> 00:38:03,928
[SPEAKER_01]: So, everything belongs to Mara.

504
00:38:05,652 --> 00:38:07,253
[SPEAKER_01]: And the problem is not you using it.

505
00:38:07,413 --> 00:38:12,717
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you can use it warily, like the the the the Veda Sutta, the Dhyana Sutta.

506
00:38:12,757 --> 00:38:13,358
[SPEAKER_01]: But what is it?

507
00:38:13,438 --> 00:38:15,619
[SPEAKER_01]: The problem is you appropriating it.

508
00:38:18,121 --> 00:38:18,261
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

509
00:38:18,281 --> 00:38:19,622
[SPEAKER_00]: You taking it to be yours.

510
00:38:19,642 --> 00:38:22,985
[SPEAKER_00]: But you're appropriately using, not the actually doing.

511
00:38:23,025 --> 00:38:26,808
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, through using, you end up appropriating it, because you're using it unwarily.

512
00:38:28,129 --> 00:38:30,971
[SPEAKER_00]: But how does this using work?

513
00:38:31,711 --> 00:38:32,192
[SPEAKER_00]: What do you mean?

514
00:38:33,600 --> 00:38:36,862
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, because there's the intention to do something.

515
00:38:37,342 --> 00:38:41,925
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, using works by doing it, without taking it to be yours.

516
00:38:46,228 --> 00:38:56,354
[SPEAKER_00]: But when you cut things up, when you slice them apart, separate, there's an intention to use them, to attempt to something.

517
00:38:58,116 --> 00:38:58,436
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

518
00:38:58,776 --> 00:38:59,556
[SPEAKER_00]: And so on and so on.

519
00:38:59,617 --> 00:39:00,037
[SPEAKER_00]: But there's no

520
00:39:01,983 --> 00:39:04,244
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no stationary position around us.

521
00:39:04,464 --> 00:39:06,965
[SPEAKER_00]: I am the self.

522
00:39:07,905 --> 00:39:09,666
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, now I'll use my intention.

523
00:39:10,366 --> 00:39:12,927
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll use my body.

524
00:39:13,107 --> 00:39:14,708
[SPEAKER_01]: No, there is no stationary position.

525
00:39:14,848 --> 00:39:22,731
[SPEAKER_01]: But, because people do regard their intentions as yours, as theirs, as belonging to them, they generate.

526
00:39:23,532 --> 00:39:25,752
[SPEAKER_01]: As a result of that, there is a sense of self.

527
00:39:26,593 --> 00:39:27,753
[SPEAKER_01]: There is a sense of center.

528
00:39:28,334 --> 00:39:29,234
[SPEAKER_01]: There is a sense of me.

529
00:39:30,688 --> 00:39:33,190
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you can then appropriate that center as well.

530
00:39:33,991 --> 00:39:41,816
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you might kind of pull out from all of these particular things, and you cannot mind, like, putrajana can be free from sensuality.

531
00:39:42,357 --> 00:39:47,441
[SPEAKER_01]: You let go of that, but still hold on to their self-view, sense of self.

532
00:39:48,161 --> 00:39:55,527
[SPEAKER_00]: Their center, you could say, in its rightful... what you can call it, rightfully, is a good person.

533
00:39:59,542 --> 00:40:04,505
[SPEAKER_00]: What about it?

534
00:40:04,885 --> 00:40:09,167
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what you can call the center, not the... Well, no, that's how you can call the appropriated center.

535
00:40:11,388 --> 00:40:19,513
[SPEAKER_01]: You stop regarding that center... You start seeing that center as depending upon things that are centered around it.

536
00:40:20,733 --> 00:40:22,454
[SPEAKER_01]: It won't be your center anymore.

537
00:40:23,315 --> 00:40:27,617
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the Buddha said, was it King Mahārāja Mahārāja Mahārāja Mahārāja Mahārāja Mahārāja

538
00:40:28,360 --> 00:40:29,161
[SPEAKER_01]: I can't remember the name.

539
00:40:29,581 --> 00:40:43,795
[SPEAKER_01]: He said, well, would the king, a king, would a king be determined as a king without his king's possessions, without his castle, without his queen, without his appointment, without all these things, without his ownership of, you know, the great armies?

540
00:40:43,975 --> 00:40:44,836
[SPEAKER_01]: He wouldn't be a king.

541
00:40:45,636 --> 00:40:46,878
[SPEAKER_01]: He might call himself a king.

542
00:40:47,558 --> 00:40:49,340
[SPEAKER_01]: If he's delusional, he won't be a king.

543
00:40:50,996 --> 00:41:00,661
[SPEAKER_01]: If nobody ever appointed him, he's not born in a family that appoints himself as king and then their offspring is king, that he appropriates externally, I'm the king, he wouldn't be a king.

544
00:41:00,821 --> 00:41:07,925
[SPEAKER_01]: So, all of these possessions, the title, everything else, are things that determine that center of me as a king.

545
00:41:09,526 --> 00:41:15,789
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you then start seeing all of these things that you're taking to be yours, as not yours, then you will fade.

546
00:41:17,905 --> 00:41:20,587
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's not I am, therefore things are mine.

547
00:41:20,627 --> 00:41:24,411
[SPEAKER_01]: It's because I take things to be mine, therefore I end up being.

548
00:41:25,271 --> 00:41:26,513
[SPEAKER_01]: But a center remains.

549
00:41:27,013 --> 00:41:34,920
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, the center remains in a sense, but again, in the same sense, you can say appearance remains, but existence ceases.

550
00:41:34,960 --> 00:41:37,142
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's only known once existence ceases.

551
00:41:37,262 --> 00:41:41,706
[SPEAKER_01]: So, once you let go of that center, you can say... you can see what sort of center remains.

552
00:41:45,127 --> 00:41:49,549
[SPEAKER_01]: But it can be a very different sense of center than to a sense of center that Putujan is used to.

553
00:41:52,651 --> 00:41:57,933
[SPEAKER_01]: Or not very different, if he was a very wise and restrained Putujanā, and then he just needed... he had a little dust in his eyes.

554
00:42:02,516 --> 00:42:07,118
[SPEAKER_01]: It depends how far off-center have you established your Putujana center.

555
00:42:10,480 --> 00:42:11,340
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

556
00:42:13,321 --> 00:42:14,782
[SPEAKER_00]: It's about coming back to the center.

557
00:42:15,539 --> 00:42:17,439
[SPEAKER_01]: Cool.

558
00:42:33,443 --> 00:42:39,324
[SPEAKER_01]: But again, if all of this being, existence, phenomena, it's sort of feeling too much,

559
00:42:40,613 --> 00:42:41,433
[SPEAKER_01]: Just restart.

560
00:42:42,253 --> 00:42:45,234
[SPEAKER_01]: And the first step is, why do you want to practice at all?

561
00:42:47,874 --> 00:42:48,955
[SPEAKER_01]: To be free from suffering.

562
00:42:51,995 --> 00:42:53,315
[SPEAKER_01]: And you can just revolve around that.

563
00:42:53,575 --> 00:42:53,815
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

564
00:42:53,875 --> 00:43:00,397
[SPEAKER_01]: Because becoming free from suffering frees you from sense of self, frees you from existence, frees you from all of it.

565
00:43:01,517 --> 00:43:04,417
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's just different approaches for different people, obviously.

566
00:43:05,258 --> 00:43:07,698
[SPEAKER_01]: Some need to come from this angle, the others from that angle.

567
00:43:08,797 --> 00:43:20,408
[SPEAKER_01]: But if it's just, you know, if it's... if you just can't help but have it, oh, this is all abstract, I just can't get my head around it, you might not necessarily have to, but you would have to get your head around something else.

568
00:43:21,749 --> 00:43:32,079
[SPEAKER_01]: Maybe not on the same intellectual level, because not all people have the same faculties, but certainly, there is no question of, oh, can I get this done with less effort?

569
00:43:34,385 --> 00:43:46,251
[SPEAKER_01]: The same amount of effort needs to be, well, virtually the same, not completely the same, but almost the same amount of effort needs to be put in by every pūtūjana, just in regard to different things or a different approach.

570
00:43:47,191 --> 00:43:50,933
[SPEAKER_01]: The effort of repetition, the effort of drilling, the effort of endurance, the effort of restraint.

571
00:43:54,615 --> 00:43:56,195
[SPEAKER_01]: But again, it's all done for the same goal.

572
00:43:56,936 --> 00:44:00,517
[SPEAKER_01]: Being free from suffering, being free from possibility to suffer, which is suffering.

573
00:44:01,478 --> 00:44:01,938
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's it.

574
00:44:06,999 --> 00:44:09,360
[SPEAKER_01]: So, my being, my sense of self, my center...

575
00:44:10,061 --> 00:44:10,381
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

576
00:44:10,901 --> 00:44:11,642
[SPEAKER_01]: Why am I doing this?

577
00:44:11,822 --> 00:44:12,762
[SPEAKER_01]: To be free from suffering.

578
00:44:12,902 --> 00:44:14,743
[SPEAKER_01]: Understand the four noble truths and that's it.

579
00:44:15,804 --> 00:44:19,946
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you start discerning all the other things that Buddha was talking about within that principle.

580
00:44:20,707 --> 00:44:20,827
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh!

581
00:44:22,027 --> 00:44:24,048
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't have to look left and right from it.

582
00:44:24,349 --> 00:44:24,869
[SPEAKER_01]: It's right there.

583
00:44:28,531 --> 00:44:29,251
[SPEAKER_01]: Why do you suffer?

584
00:44:34,054 --> 00:44:34,674
[SPEAKER_01]: What is suffering?

585
00:44:34,854 --> 00:44:35,515
[SPEAKER_01]: To what extent?

586
00:44:36,699 --> 00:44:38,659
[SPEAKER_01]: When does disagreeable become suffering?

587
00:44:40,000 --> 00:44:41,280
[SPEAKER_01]: Is disagreeable suffering?

588
00:44:42,140 --> 00:44:44,780
[SPEAKER_01]: Can you have disagreeable without actually suffering on account of it?

589
00:44:50,201 --> 00:44:51,842
[SPEAKER_01]: Quite useful questions to contemplate on.

590
00:44:54,642 --> 00:45:01,563
[SPEAKER_01]: Because most people, disagreeable, in the same sense, again, there you go, I just said, it's all the same, the rewards are on the same principles.

591
00:45:02,123 --> 00:45:05,864
[SPEAKER_01]: In the same sense, I said, you can have appearance without existence.

592
00:45:07,122 --> 00:45:08,643
[SPEAKER_01]: But you can't have existence without appearance.

593
00:45:09,084 --> 00:45:11,326
[SPEAKER_01]: You can have disagreeable without suffering.

594
00:45:11,886 --> 00:45:13,628
[SPEAKER_01]: But you can't have suffering without disagreeable.

595
00:45:18,572 --> 00:45:20,734
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't have suffering without disagreeable.

596
00:45:21,955 --> 00:45:23,777
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't suffer if there's nothing disagreeable.

597
00:45:26,747 --> 00:45:28,388
[SPEAKER_01]: But you can have disagreeable without suffering.

598
00:45:29,048 --> 00:45:40,173
[SPEAKER_01]: So, and it's because you can't suffer without a disagreeable experience, that's why people by default assume, ah, removing disagreeable means removing suffering.

599
00:45:40,574 --> 00:45:41,794
[SPEAKER_01]: But that is overstepping it.

600
00:45:42,454 --> 00:45:44,535
[SPEAKER_01]: That is going into the domain that doesn't belong to you.

601
00:45:46,376 --> 00:45:51,879
[SPEAKER_01]: The domain of agreeable and disagreeable, things that just arise as agreeable and disagreeable, that's not yours.

602
00:45:53,422 --> 00:45:55,082
[SPEAKER_01]: Suffering in regard to it is yours.

603
00:45:55,543 --> 00:46:00,004
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's your attitude in regards to agreeable or disagreeable that's making you suffer.

604
00:46:02,104 --> 00:46:06,845
[SPEAKER_01]: So, whenever there is disagreeable, you suffer because of your attitude in regard to disagreeable.

605
00:46:07,506 --> 00:46:10,006
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in that sense, it's the middle way.

606
00:46:10,026 --> 00:46:13,927
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the middle way because you don't go and just remove disagreeable altogether.

607
00:46:13,967 --> 00:46:16,068
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't, because you're not in control of that.

608
00:46:16,188 --> 00:46:16,808
[SPEAKER_01]: You never were.

609
00:46:18,135 --> 00:46:23,319
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't stop eyes from seeing, ears from hearing, mind from thinking.

610
00:46:25,060 --> 00:46:30,824
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, most certainly, you cannot order it to only see pleasant things, and hear pleasant things, and think pleasant things.

611
00:46:30,844 --> 00:46:31,384
[SPEAKER_01]: It's impossible.

612
00:46:31,825 --> 00:46:32,005
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

613
00:46:32,045 --> 00:46:33,046
[SPEAKER_01]: Because even pleasant...

614
00:46:34,116 --> 00:46:36,198
[SPEAKER_01]: It can be a source of disagreeable.

615
00:46:36,939 --> 00:46:43,824
[SPEAKER_01]: If you start thinking about more pleasant, which is, you know, not what you're currently experiencing, well, that's a disagreeable experience.

616
00:46:44,345 --> 00:46:49,349
[SPEAKER_01]: You created that disagreeable discrepancy, and now you suffer and encounter it, because you lust for more.

617
00:46:50,350 --> 00:46:51,951
[SPEAKER_01]: What you have is far from enough.

618
00:46:52,071 --> 00:47:01,960
[SPEAKER_01]: You used to be enough, but because the mind was unrestrained, you created discrepancy upon which you got established, and now you just crave, need, depend on more.

619
00:47:02,654 --> 00:47:08,377
[SPEAKER_01]: And you become very unhappy, very, very affected by suffering, in regard to very pleasant experiences.

620
00:47:11,178 --> 00:47:17,581
[SPEAKER_00]: So, your attitude towards disagreeable and agreeable... Yeah, is what basically makes you suffer.

621
00:47:17,761 --> 00:47:20,802
[SPEAKER_01]: And that attitude is actually the craving.

622
00:47:22,163 --> 00:47:25,444
[SPEAKER_00]: Not wanting disagreeable and wanting pleasure.

623
00:47:25,664 --> 00:47:26,365
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

624
00:47:26,545 --> 00:47:27,245
[SPEAKER_01]: Overstepping it.

625
00:47:32,975 --> 00:47:35,617
[SPEAKER_00]: So, changing the attitude being... Yeah.

626
00:47:35,937 --> 00:47:41,740
[SPEAKER_01]: Knowing, but first knowing that you should stop being concerned about the actual agreeable and disagreeable.

627
00:47:41,760 --> 00:47:43,821
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you don't go trying to change the circumstances.

628
00:47:44,762 --> 00:47:52,206
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, of course, if you live in the middle of the city, in a horrible environment, and you want to become an arahant, well, you have to change those circumstances.

629
00:47:52,306 --> 00:47:54,087
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's not what I'm talking about.

630
00:47:54,567 --> 00:47:58,810
[SPEAKER_01]: What I'm talking about is if, I don't know, somebody said something that upset you.

631
00:48:00,819 --> 00:48:02,240
[SPEAKER_01]: You're responsible for the upset.

632
00:48:02,380 --> 00:48:04,082
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not responsible for what's being said.

633
00:48:06,183 --> 00:48:07,564
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you suffer, it's on you.

634
00:48:08,745 --> 00:48:08,925
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

635
00:48:08,965 --> 00:48:11,988
[SPEAKER_01]: Because suffering is present when craving is present.

636
00:48:12,068 --> 00:48:14,009
[SPEAKER_01]: Craving in regard to agreeable or disagreeable.

637
00:48:14,990 --> 00:48:15,250
[SPEAKER_01]: Not...

638
00:48:15,831 --> 00:48:19,053
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not that suffering is present when agreeable or disagreeable is present.

639
00:48:19,193 --> 00:48:19,374
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

640
00:48:19,734 --> 00:48:22,516
[SPEAKER_01]: You can have agreeable and disagreeable without actually suffering.

641
00:48:23,757 --> 00:48:24,077
[SPEAKER_01]: At all.

642
00:48:25,438 --> 00:48:27,220
[SPEAKER_01]: So, somebody said something that upsets you,

643
00:48:28,626 --> 00:48:41,849
[SPEAKER_01]: Default, putuginal response is to, well, either get upset and say something back that will hurt them, or, if perhaps you have a bit more restraint, then, well, let's go to them and talk, or wanting their apology.

644
00:48:41,909 --> 00:48:45,110
[SPEAKER_01]: You want to affect it on that external level where it came from.

645
00:48:46,670 --> 00:48:47,791
[SPEAKER_01]: But actually, you're not.

646
00:48:47,951 --> 00:48:50,231
[SPEAKER_01]: You're, again, as I said, you're overstepping it.

647
00:48:50,351 --> 00:48:51,291
[SPEAKER_01]: You're misplacing it.

648
00:48:52,012 --> 00:48:53,772
[SPEAKER_01]: That was not the suffering to begin with.

649
00:48:54,747 --> 00:49:00,831
[SPEAKER_01]: It was your attitude in regard to that which has come your way in terms of somebody saying something.

650
00:49:01,771 --> 00:49:07,554
[SPEAKER_01]: And the first thing you recognize is like, well, there's always going to be something disagreeable in the world.

651
00:49:07,735 --> 00:49:16,019
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if I get all of the people to be on my side, to agree with me, to always sing praises of me, something else will always be disagreeable.

652
00:49:16,760 --> 00:49:20,122
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means there's always something else is going to come your way that you're not going to like.

653
00:49:21,362 --> 00:49:21,863
[SPEAKER_01]: Eventually,

654
00:49:23,222 --> 00:49:24,303
[SPEAKER_01]: Aging, sickness and death.

655
00:49:24,543 --> 00:49:25,004
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's it.

656
00:49:25,824 --> 00:49:28,387
[SPEAKER_01]: And, you know, there is no sugar coating that.

657
00:49:30,649 --> 00:49:32,050
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you recognize it's inevitable.

658
00:49:33,511 --> 00:49:38,015
[SPEAKER_01]: And, but then, again, see, oh, oh my goodness, it's all, it's all depressing now.

659
00:49:38,776 --> 00:49:41,918
[SPEAKER_01]: Everything, everything is inevitable in that sense.

660
00:49:41,978 --> 00:49:45,161
[SPEAKER_01]: Everything is just going to come to a horrible, disagreeable end.

661
00:49:46,562 --> 00:49:49,685
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you could see people falling into despair, if they start thinking like that.

662
00:49:49,765 --> 00:49:50,526
[SPEAKER_01]: And then imagine

663
00:49:51,705 --> 00:49:54,986
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, imagine the extent of the Buddha's discovery.

664
00:49:55,467 --> 00:50:06,871
[SPEAKER_01]: Within that, he managed to discern that none of that is a cause for the pain that you feel, or you might feel, or the suffering.

665
00:50:07,532 --> 00:50:08,172
[SPEAKER_01]: None of that.

666
00:50:08,312 --> 00:50:12,254
[SPEAKER_01]: So, imagine that, like seeing through these just layers of identical glass.

667
00:50:13,014 --> 00:50:17,736
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, you start discerning them without any external input, without...

668
00:50:17,916 --> 00:50:19,777
[SPEAKER_01]: He didn't have another Buddha's guidance.

669
00:50:20,727 --> 00:50:22,728
[SPEAKER_01]: He saw it from the inside, which is what?

670
00:50:22,848 --> 00:50:23,488
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is a mirror.

671
00:50:23,648 --> 00:50:25,489
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is, you know, that's why he's the Buddha.

672
00:50:27,549 --> 00:50:38,233
[SPEAKER_01]: It's easier for us now, because we have it, like, laid down, pointed out, you know, outlined by the Buddha and other arahants and so on, and the suttas, mainly, only.

673
00:50:40,173 --> 00:50:44,755
[SPEAKER_01]: So you then, now you know what to look for from the inside.

674
00:50:49,268 --> 00:50:56,793
[SPEAKER_01]: So, again, the whole point is basically, when you recognize, oh, wow, the freedom lies within that, right through the center of that.

675
00:50:57,813 --> 00:50:58,574
[SPEAKER_01]: Complete freedom.

676
00:51:01,455 --> 00:51:03,196
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the problem was never disagreeable.

677
00:51:04,057 --> 00:51:08,280
[SPEAKER_01]: No matter how disagreeable it was, even if it was highway robbers sawing your limbs and so on.

678
00:51:08,960 --> 00:51:09,881
[SPEAKER_01]: That was never the issue.

679
00:51:10,261 --> 00:51:12,082
[SPEAKER_01]: The issue was the fact that you were touched by it.

680
00:51:12,402 --> 00:51:14,363
[SPEAKER_01]: And you were touched by it because you were picking it up.

681
00:51:15,064 --> 00:51:16,104
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you were touching it.

682
00:51:17,511 --> 00:51:21,893
[SPEAKER_01]: There's a hot stove, and you put a hand on it, always, and you don't know how to stop it.

683
00:51:23,114 --> 00:51:24,495
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you start blaming the stove.

684
00:51:25,796 --> 00:51:29,278
[SPEAKER_01]: And you want to try to remove it, abolish the stove, but it's not yours, and you can't do it.

685
00:51:31,099 --> 00:51:38,863
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, it's a bit simplifying it a bit, but basically, craving, as a mental aptitude, is the cause.

686
00:51:39,283 --> 00:51:44,066
[SPEAKER_01]: So, simultaneously, and that's it, that's why it's patīcamo pādre, it's a simultaneous presence.

687
00:51:45,232 --> 00:51:48,954
[SPEAKER_01]: Simultaneous dependence.

688
00:51:49,034 --> 00:52:01,082
[SPEAKER_01]: It's inconceivable to have dukkha present, dukkha arisen, if there is no, at the same time, to the same extent, dhamma present, dhamma arisen.

689
00:52:01,902 --> 00:52:04,103
[SPEAKER_01]: Absence of dhamma, absence of dukkha.

690
00:52:04,504 --> 00:52:06,505
[SPEAKER_01]: Presence of dhamma, presence of dukkha.

691
00:52:06,525 --> 00:52:12,268
[SPEAKER_00]: Could you have a... could you have an intention of non-breathing?

692
00:52:12,689 --> 00:52:13,009
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

693
00:52:13,669 --> 00:52:14,650
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what you guys...

694
00:52:17,316 --> 00:52:20,557
[SPEAKER_01]: But not when there is no craving, because then that intention would be craving.

695
00:52:22,397 --> 00:52:25,558
[SPEAKER_01]: The intention of non-craving has to be in regard to craving.

696
00:52:29,099 --> 00:52:30,179
[SPEAKER_00]: But when there's no craving?

697
00:52:30,219 --> 00:52:33,560
[SPEAKER_01]: When there is no craving, you intending to not crave would be craving.

698
00:52:34,020 --> 00:52:34,180
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

699
00:52:35,660 --> 00:52:37,241
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why, how did you cross the shore?

700
00:52:37,961 --> 00:52:44,282
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, by not fighting the waves, by not kind of going backwards, and by not standing still.

701
00:52:44,522 --> 00:52:47,023
[SPEAKER_01]: So, at the time, you intend riding.

702
00:52:57,884 --> 00:53:08,868
[SPEAKER_01]: But again, don't think, which I'm sure you would and everybody would, including myself, when I first read it, that it's like you need to moment, like, catch the intention in the moment and then counter it rightly.

703
00:53:09,188 --> 00:53:09,909
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not like that.

704
00:53:11,049 --> 00:53:15,151
[SPEAKER_01]: It's basically recognizing the general intention and then relinquishing it.

705
00:53:17,972 --> 00:53:21,533
[SPEAKER_01]: And you can always recognize the general intention by being aware of dukkha.

706
00:53:23,449 --> 00:53:23,929
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's it.

707
00:53:24,009 --> 00:53:26,931
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why suffering and freedom from suffering, that's all the Buddha teaches.

708
00:53:28,252 --> 00:53:30,673
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if there's presence of dukkha, you know there's craving.

709
00:53:30,693 --> 00:53:31,534
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't need to look for it.

710
00:53:32,254 --> 00:53:33,155
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, but where is it?

711
00:53:33,235 --> 00:53:33,715
[SPEAKER_01]: How is it?

712
00:53:34,756 --> 00:53:34,936
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

713
00:53:35,136 --> 00:53:36,276
[SPEAKER_01]: I suffer means craving is there.

714
00:53:36,877 --> 00:53:45,682
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how can I, so to speak, tune my attitude whereby amidst disagreeable, I'm not suffering?

715
00:53:46,560 --> 00:53:46,820
[SPEAKER_01]: At all.

716
00:53:47,400 --> 00:53:57,503
[SPEAKER_01]: Not because I'm running away from disagreeable, not because I'm ignoring disagreeable, not because I'm psychologizing the disagreeable and explaining myself, you know, ah, it's because of my childhood or this or that.

717
00:53:57,783 --> 00:53:57,963
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

718
00:53:58,443 --> 00:53:59,843
[SPEAKER_01]: Because I don't crave in regard to it.

719
00:54:00,363 --> 00:54:01,364
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't fear the pain.

720
00:54:01,764 --> 00:54:02,784
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't lust the pleasure.

721
00:54:05,845 --> 00:54:09,406
[SPEAKER_01]: Means you don't crave, you don't overshoot, you don't step in, you don't run away.

722
00:54:10,126 --> 00:54:12,726
[SPEAKER_01]: Means you don't react in regard to agreeable or disagreeable.

723
00:54:14,847 --> 00:54:15,567
[SPEAKER_01]: Means you don't suffer.

724
00:54:24,965 --> 00:54:28,568
[SPEAKER_01]: And again, disagreeable and agreeable, it's not set in stone.

725
00:54:29,509 --> 00:54:31,811
[SPEAKER_01]: Can be... can arise in different extents.

726
00:54:33,252 --> 00:54:34,033
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's another thing.

727
00:54:34,673 --> 00:54:41,079
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you might... you might get used to a certain level of disagreeable and that ceases to cause you pain, suffering mentally.

728
00:54:41,639 --> 00:54:45,702
[SPEAKER_01]: But then the level of disagreeable increases and then you start suffering again.

729
00:54:47,444 --> 00:54:53,349
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you just... you're not training in regard to the extent of disagreeable, which is why you don't have to go and torture yourself.

730
00:54:54,435 --> 00:54:56,497
[SPEAKER_01]: As some ascetics thought, they would have to.

731
00:54:57,438 --> 00:55:00,461
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're not training in regard to the extent of disagreeable.

732
00:55:01,021 --> 00:55:03,283
[SPEAKER_01]: You're training in regard to the nature of disagreeable.

733
00:55:04,705 --> 00:55:10,390
[SPEAKER_01]: And you stop feeling the nature of disagreeable, the extent becomes irrelevant.

734
00:55:11,138 --> 00:55:16,122
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, to the point of highway rollers chopping your limbs one by one with a blunt saw.

735
00:55:17,363 --> 00:55:18,824
[SPEAKER_01]: And you still won't be suffering.

736
00:55:18,945 --> 00:55:20,226
[SPEAKER_01]: It was very disagreeable.

737
00:55:20,366 --> 00:55:21,807
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, extremely disagreeable.

738
00:55:22,508 --> 00:55:33,797
[SPEAKER_01]: But mentally, you actually won't be fearing it or suffering on account of it.

739
00:55:33,817 --> 00:55:37,740
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, death you've overcome.

740
00:55:37,760 --> 00:55:39,101
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, you overcome death.

741
00:55:41,503 --> 00:55:42,764
[SPEAKER_01]: The utmost disagreeable.

742
00:55:54,033 --> 00:56:00,458
[SPEAKER_01]: As I was saying before, when Kuntūjana was touched by suffering, he suffers on account of that as well.

743
00:56:04,453 --> 00:56:06,715
[SPEAKER_01]: Because he can't prevent it, and he knows no escape.

744
00:56:07,375 --> 00:56:13,440
[SPEAKER_01]: When sotāpanna, or an ārya, is touched by suffering, he doesn't suffer under that.

745
00:56:14,061 --> 00:56:18,785
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, fully developed ārya, who becomes an ārahant, he's not touched even in the first place.

746
00:56:20,907 --> 00:56:25,250
[SPEAKER_00]: So, for sotāpanna, he can suffer, but only to a certain extent.

747
00:56:25,330 --> 00:56:26,271
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

748
00:56:26,531 --> 00:56:32,936
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is, well, he can suffer to the extent of seven little grains of sand, in comparison to the Himalayas.

749
00:56:33,968 --> 00:56:37,949
