923
01:08:57,045 --> 01:09:03,206
[SPEAKER_01]: Whatever suffering, on account of disagreeable that arises for Putujanā, he suffers on account of that twice, on another level.

924
01:09:03,566 --> 01:09:05,587
[SPEAKER_01]: And there's a simile of the... how was it?

925
01:09:05,767 --> 01:09:07,588
[SPEAKER_01]: The two errors.

926
01:09:07,608 --> 01:09:13,689
[SPEAKER_01]: Man gets hit by one error, and then he's struck by that pain, and then he gets struck by another error on account of that.

927
01:09:14,529 --> 01:09:20,111
[SPEAKER_01]: And that doesn't happen to an ādiya-sāvaka, to a noble disciple, basically.

928
01:09:20,651 --> 01:09:23,372
[SPEAKER_01]: He's struck by one error of suffering, but he knows escape.

929
01:09:24,284 --> 01:09:26,945
[SPEAKER_01]: from that pain that has arisen on account of that error.

930
01:09:27,386 --> 01:09:31,507
[SPEAKER_01]: So, he's not struck by the second error of panic and fear and distraught.

931
01:09:31,788 --> 01:09:33,408
[SPEAKER_00]: And escape is not the understanding.

932
01:09:33,629 --> 01:09:35,109
[SPEAKER_01]: Escape is fully understood.

933
01:09:35,289 --> 01:09:36,490
[SPEAKER_01]: Before, that's the insight.

934
01:09:36,530 --> 01:09:37,911
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why he became a sotāpanna.

935
01:09:38,531 --> 01:09:45,094
[SPEAKER_01]: So, no matter how painful, how sufferable things arise, he won't suffer on account of that.

936
01:09:47,055 --> 01:09:50,016
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, for an hour... and then, if he pursues it fully, then they won't even arise.

937
01:09:50,417 --> 01:09:52,958
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, it will only be like disagreeable arising, agreeable arising.

938
01:09:53,927 --> 01:09:59,610
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, the Buddha says, oh, sometimes he... a person comes and asks him for the instruction.

939
01:10:00,311 --> 01:10:01,331
[SPEAKER_01]: He tells them what to do.

940
01:10:01,351 --> 01:10:02,832
[SPEAKER_01]: Then they go and do it.

941
01:10:02,872 --> 01:10:04,453
[SPEAKER_01]: He says, Tathāgata is pleased.

942
01:10:06,074 --> 01:10:10,316
[SPEAKER_01]: If he finds that agreeable, then another man comes and the Buddha teaches him for three more hours.

943
01:10:10,376 --> 01:10:11,817
[SPEAKER_01]: But then he goes and it's completely the opposite.

944
01:10:11,917 --> 01:10:13,378
[SPEAKER_01]: He said, Tathāgata is displeased.

945
01:10:13,778 --> 01:10:14,959
[SPEAKER_01]: He finds that disagreeable.

946
01:10:15,660 --> 01:10:16,480
[SPEAKER_01]: They said, but none of...

947
01:10:17,525 --> 01:10:26,650
[SPEAKER_01]: Neither of those accounts do make the target person suffer, or get upset, or get elated with great joy, because somebody's following his instruction.

948
01:10:26,851 --> 01:10:30,893
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you say, oh, this is agreeable, that's suitable, that's not suitable, that's disagreeable.

949
01:10:39,078 --> 01:10:40,118
[SPEAKER_00]: So, let's see.

950
01:10:41,159 --> 01:10:46,122
[SPEAKER_00]: These terms, I don't know if we go through all of them, but the dhamma is

951
01:10:47,484 --> 01:10:51,105
[SPEAKER_00]: Upanāyaka, leading to, bringing near.

952
01:10:51,565 --> 01:10:52,025
[SPEAKER_00]: Leading on?

953
01:10:52,425 --> 01:10:54,245
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, I just wrote down some translation.

954
01:10:54,345 --> 01:10:54,926
[SPEAKER_00]: Uh-huh, uh-huh.

955
01:10:54,966 --> 01:10:57,086
[SPEAKER_00]: Leading to, bringing near, leading on.

956
01:10:57,846 --> 01:11:08,828
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it's basically... you need to see it, you know, to know it, to put it simply.

957
01:11:09,189 --> 01:11:16,070
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, you will develop the dhamma that you're trying to practice by becoming sattva and then an arahant.

958
01:11:17,073 --> 01:11:22,696
[SPEAKER_01]: Not by thinking you understood dhamma, but at the same time knowing that you're still putujana.

959
01:11:24,557 --> 01:11:35,623
[SPEAKER_01]: So, understanding of the dhamma leads you onwards, further and further and further, or rather closer and closer and closer to the goal, further and further away from being putujana.

960
01:11:36,584 --> 01:11:39,585
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's not like, oh, I understood the dhamma and that's it.

961
01:11:41,687 --> 01:11:45,809
[SPEAKER_01]: I keep my old ways of being putujana or whatever else, but I understood the dhamma.

962
01:11:47,074 --> 01:11:48,054
[SPEAKER_01]: That's not the dhamma then.

963
01:11:48,795 --> 01:11:54,116
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you were to gain any understanding of the dhamma, you would then recognize, oh, there is more.

964
01:11:55,156 --> 01:11:57,957
[SPEAKER_01]: Then you get the understanding of that more and then, oh no, there is more.

965
01:11:58,997 --> 01:12:00,238
[SPEAKER_01]: There is more, there is more, there is more.

966
01:12:00,498 --> 01:12:02,619
[SPEAKER_01]: Until the other hand says, there is no more.

967
01:12:02,959 --> 01:12:04,019
[SPEAKER_01]: Everything has been completed.

968
01:12:04,979 --> 01:12:07,120
[SPEAKER_01]: The task, you know, the burden has been laid down.

969
01:12:07,160 --> 01:12:07,760
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no more.

970
01:12:08,820 --> 01:12:11,061
[SPEAKER_01]: So it led him onwards until he reached the goal.

971
01:12:12,724 --> 01:12:14,686
[SPEAKER_00]: Didn't he recognize suffering?

972
01:12:15,287 --> 01:12:15,587
[SPEAKER_00]: Sorry?

973
01:12:15,707 --> 01:12:17,269
[SPEAKER_00]: He recognized suffering.

974
01:12:17,529 --> 01:12:18,931
[SPEAKER_00]: And he thought he freed himself from it.

975
01:12:18,951 --> 01:12:19,672
[SPEAKER_00]: It's all nature.

976
01:12:19,832 --> 01:12:20,613
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

977
01:12:20,853 --> 01:12:21,634
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's the thing.

978
01:12:21,654 --> 01:12:25,838
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, again, you can... and there were different dhammas, different teachings in the Buddha's day.

979
01:12:25,878 --> 01:12:28,081
[SPEAKER_01]: He said, well, not all of them are actually valid.

980
01:12:29,319 --> 01:12:29,719
[SPEAKER_01]: And why?

981
01:12:29,879 --> 01:12:34,022
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, yeah, they would not be leading you to the to the final goal of freedom.

982
01:12:34,842 --> 01:12:41,906
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if your dhamma says, oh, understanding of this will free you from suffering, then you understand that, but you're not free from suffering.

983
01:12:42,026 --> 01:12:45,227
[SPEAKER_01]: That's either the wrong dhamma or you haven't understood it.

984
01:12:46,768 --> 01:12:51,050
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the faith in the Buddha's teaching has to come first.

985
01:12:51,170 --> 01:12:54,532
[SPEAKER_01]: In the same sense, you will have faith in a doctor when he prescribes your medicine.

986
01:12:56,410 --> 01:13:02,448
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you don't have faith in the Buddha's teaching, if you were to start practicing, you're not free from suffering, but you understood it.

987
01:13:03,636 --> 01:13:06,138
[SPEAKER_01]: You think, okay, I understood this, but it's not freedom from suffering.

988
01:13:06,218 --> 01:13:07,158
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll go to something else.

989
01:13:07,799 --> 01:13:10,721
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you have faith in the Buddha's teaching, then you'll question yourself.

990
01:13:10,821 --> 01:13:12,442
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not going to place the blame on the teaching.

991
01:13:12,462 --> 01:13:16,225
[SPEAKER_01]: You'll say, no, I have faith that this is right, yet I'm not free from suffering.

992
01:13:16,765 --> 01:13:18,386
[SPEAKER_01]: Means, I haven't fully understood it.

993
01:13:19,107 --> 01:13:20,348
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you make more effort and more.

994
01:13:20,588 --> 01:13:21,709
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why it's a bit risky.

995
01:13:22,209 --> 01:13:29,674
[SPEAKER_01]: If you do pick up a wrong dhamma, a wrong teaching, a wrong instruction, even quoted as Buddhism, you'll end up wasting.

996
01:13:29,894 --> 01:13:31,055
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the Buddha says it was one of

997
01:13:32,977 --> 01:13:37,379
[SPEAKER_01]: And certainly, in terms of demerit, it's presenting as dhamma that which is not dhamma.

998
01:13:37,939 --> 01:13:45,142
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, see, you can obstruct many beings in finding the true dhamma on account of something you just said.

999
01:13:45,822 --> 01:13:48,724
[SPEAKER_01]: That kind of, you know, gain exposure and people heard it.

1000
01:13:49,524 --> 01:13:50,544
[SPEAKER_01]: And again, if you say...

1001
01:13:50,564 --> 01:13:52,585
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

1002
01:13:52,605 --> 01:13:55,907
[SPEAKER_01]: On account of faith of the Buddha, they thought what you were saying was right.

1003
01:13:56,187 --> 01:14:02,089
[SPEAKER_01]: They followed it for 50 years, and they haven't... they're still puthujjana, and it's directly a result of what you basically misinterpret.

1004
01:14:02,649 --> 01:14:07,471
[SPEAKER_01]: Obviously, that doesn't mean if you say something by accident, that somebody misunderstands.

1005
01:14:07,611 --> 01:14:13,754
[SPEAKER_01]: Means, you deep down knowing you're puthujjana, and you go out and teach and tell people how to practice dhamma.

1006
01:14:15,260 --> 01:14:17,501
[SPEAKER_01]: It feels like, no, but I'm doing it out of good intention.

1007
01:14:17,581 --> 01:14:19,322
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, good, misguided intention.

1008
01:14:19,382 --> 01:14:20,503
[SPEAKER_01]: And is that misguidedness?

1009
01:14:21,403 --> 01:14:22,404
[SPEAKER_01]: It's gonna cost you a lot.

1010
01:14:22,924 --> 01:14:23,104
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

1011
01:14:23,224 --> 01:14:24,765
[SPEAKER_00]: Or could cost you a lot.

1012
01:14:24,805 --> 01:14:27,127
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it will affect others a lot more.

1013
01:14:27,487 --> 01:14:29,148
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

1014
01:14:31,029 --> 01:14:35,491
[SPEAKER_01]: And let alone if somebody intentionally, like fully, misrepresents.

1015
01:14:36,032 --> 01:14:37,132
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you do hear it a lot.

1016
01:14:38,233 --> 01:14:41,935
[SPEAKER_00]: A dhamma talk, where someone will talk and say, ends will start off.

1017
01:14:42,895 --> 01:14:43,435
[SPEAKER_00]: By saying...

1018
01:14:43,856 --> 01:14:47,438
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know anything, but let me tell you how it is.

1019
01:14:48,759 --> 01:14:49,819
[SPEAKER_01]: It absolves them a bit.

1020
01:14:50,440 --> 01:14:51,521
[SPEAKER_01]: It absolves them a bit.

1021
01:14:51,821 --> 01:14:58,745
[SPEAKER_01]: Because they say, I don't know, I'm still a patujana, but I'll give you an hour-long dhamma talk, which is a contradiction in term.

1022
01:14:58,765 --> 01:15:04,189
[SPEAKER_01]: It wouldn't be a dhamma talk then, because talking about the dhamma is not a dhamma, but it's a different topic.

1023
01:15:04,289 --> 01:15:10,373
[SPEAKER_01]: It absolves them a bit, because then it's a responsibility on those listeners to then, hold on, he said he doesn't know,

1024
01:15:11,366 --> 01:15:16,049
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, I mean, I might stay and listen, but I'll certainly not take any great heed in it.

1025
01:15:16,910 --> 01:15:20,112
[SPEAKER_01]: But if they ignore that and then carry on, it's a bit more than that.

1026
01:15:20,152 --> 01:15:22,634
[SPEAKER_01]: But many people never, you know, they don't say that often enough.

1027
01:15:22,714 --> 01:15:25,756
[SPEAKER_01]: They don't say often enough, hey, I don't know, but I'll talk.

1028
01:15:26,276 --> 01:15:26,877
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll talk to you.

1029
01:15:27,777 --> 01:15:31,420
[SPEAKER_01]: But I think they end up just getting used to talking and end up just talking.

1030
01:15:31,700 --> 01:15:34,302
[SPEAKER_01]: And then people end up thinking, oh, they know what they're talking about.

1031
01:15:36,932 --> 01:15:43,677
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why a person who does want to learn and place faith, needs to be very careful where they place their faith in.

1032
01:15:45,879 --> 01:15:50,823
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why you never compromise your intelligence, reasoning, discernment, even on account of faith.

1033
01:15:51,423 --> 01:16:04,013
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you discern, you use what you... any tools you have, any grains of wisdom you have, you get them all together, and then choose wisely where you place your faith as something that's least likely going to be wrong.

1034
01:16:04,053 --> 01:16:05,474
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you don't know what's right, what's wrong.

1035
01:16:06,497 --> 01:16:11,158
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what is least likely to be mistaken down the road?

1036
01:16:13,139 --> 01:16:15,339
[SPEAKER_01]: And even there, you tread carefully, step by step.

1037
01:16:15,739 --> 01:16:31,883
[SPEAKER_01]: Obviously, then, you know, if you did place it rightly or least wrongly, you will start to descend more and more, which means more and more grains of wisdom will be adding up, which means more and more of the right criteria will be shaping up, which means faster and faster you can go.

1038
01:16:36,723 --> 01:16:39,765
[SPEAKER_00]: And this... this is a strange one.

1039
01:16:40,025 --> 01:16:40,865
[SPEAKER_00]: Ehipassika.

1040
01:16:41,525 --> 01:16:42,226
[SPEAKER_00]: Ehipassika.

1041
01:16:42,866 --> 01:16:44,407
[SPEAKER_00]: Inviting everyone to come and see.

1042
01:16:44,987 --> 01:16:46,128
[SPEAKER_00]: Or open to all.

1043
01:16:46,928 --> 01:16:48,749
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I think it's a bit illiberal.

1044
01:16:49,129 --> 01:16:50,070
[SPEAKER_01]: It means come and see.

1045
01:16:50,370 --> 01:16:50,850
[SPEAKER_01]: Come and see.

1046
01:16:50,870 --> 01:16:51,250
[SPEAKER_01]: Basically.

1047
01:16:52,411 --> 01:16:53,151
[SPEAKER_01]: Just come and see.

1048
01:16:54,132 --> 01:16:54,652
[SPEAKER_00]: A dharma.

1049
01:16:54,792 --> 01:16:55,012
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

1050
01:16:55,392 --> 01:16:56,253
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I want to really...

1051
01:16:56,793 --> 01:16:58,374
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm interested in what you're teaching and stuff.

1052
01:16:58,474 --> 01:17:02,956
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, if you really... Basically, the teaching pertains only to being done.

1053
01:17:03,801 --> 01:17:07,483
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, as I said, there is a difference between about the dhamma and the dhamma.

1054
01:17:07,803 --> 01:17:09,624
[SPEAKER_01]: And about the dhamma is not the dhamma.

1055
01:17:10,205 --> 01:17:11,806
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's a very important distinction.

1056
01:17:12,786 --> 01:17:19,350
[SPEAKER_01]: So, people who are about the dhamma, you tell them, no, come and see here, I do it, and that is what the dhamma is not what you...

1057
01:17:19,710 --> 01:17:26,254
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if you're parroting the very same phrases and words, you're not actually trying to see it for yourself.

1058
01:17:26,694 --> 01:17:29,656
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, it's not the dhamma, it's about the dhamma, and that's not the dhamma.

1059
01:17:30,497 --> 01:17:32,218
[SPEAKER_01]: So, come and see it, and then

1060
01:17:33,452 --> 01:17:35,554
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the only way to know it, is to come and see it.

1061
01:17:37,035 --> 01:17:38,036
[SPEAKER_01]: So, eh, hibāsiko.

1062
01:17:38,876 --> 01:17:39,777
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, hibāsiko.

1063
01:17:40,037 --> 01:17:40,298
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

1064
01:17:43,280 --> 01:17:49,145
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, saṃdhītika, meaning to feel visible.

1065
01:17:50,125 --> 01:17:51,286
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, come and see for yourself.

1066
01:17:51,346 --> 01:17:51,747
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, come.

1067
01:17:51,767 --> 01:17:52,507
[SPEAKER_01]: You start doing it.

1068
01:17:53,088 --> 01:17:55,530
[SPEAKER_01]: The more you do it, the more you see, the more you see, the more you can do.

1069
01:17:57,733 --> 01:18:02,517
[SPEAKER_01]: But, if it's about the dhamma, it can often, like, have a... have a lid on it quite quickly.

1070
01:18:02,977 --> 01:18:04,559
[SPEAKER_01]: Ah, okay, that's what dhamma is.

1071
01:18:05,219 --> 01:18:10,123
[SPEAKER_01]: But you're not really making that personal effort, internally, to see it and understand it and free yourself from suffering.

1072
01:18:10,163 --> 01:18:10,924
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's it.

1073
01:18:11,324 --> 01:18:11,925
[SPEAKER_01]: It stops there.

1074
01:18:14,327 --> 01:18:17,049
[SPEAKER_01]: Ah, dhamma is this, this and that, and I can talk about it.

1075
01:18:17,249 --> 01:18:21,152
[SPEAKER_01]: I can talk from different angles about it, but... it's kind of frozen.

1076
01:18:21,572 --> 01:18:22,613
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is why it's not the dhamma.

1077
01:18:23,014 --> 01:18:25,235
[SPEAKER_01]: The only dhamma, the real dhamma, is the dhamma that does... that

1078
01:18:27,025 --> 01:18:32,187
[SPEAKER_01]: If it's visible, they can be seen individually by the wise and those who make the effort.

1079
01:18:33,567 --> 01:18:34,747
[SPEAKER_01]: And the concept...

1080
01:18:34,867 --> 01:18:35,147
[SPEAKER_01]: Sorry?

1081
01:18:35,187 --> 01:18:35,568
[SPEAKER_01]: Timeless.

1082
01:18:35,588 --> 01:18:36,448
[SPEAKER_01]: About timeless, yeah.

1083
01:18:38,028 --> 01:18:39,129
[SPEAKER_01]: Means it's simultaneous.

1084
01:18:39,189 --> 01:18:40,769
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't pertain to your past, your future.

1085
01:18:41,349 --> 01:18:41,849
[SPEAKER_01]: Basically...

1086
01:18:41,869 --> 01:18:47,031
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, I don't want to say it's always in the present, because there's no choice about that.

1087
01:18:47,471 --> 01:18:50,432
[SPEAKER_01]: But basically, timeless means it doesn't pertain to time, literally.

1088
01:18:51,895 --> 01:18:55,878
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the principles of dhamma are right there, present.

1089
01:18:56,659 --> 01:19:07,868
[SPEAKER_01]: And your past is within those principles, your future is within those principles, and your present is already within those principles of the present dhamma, the universals of your experience, the nature of it.

1090
01:19:20,252 --> 01:19:24,774
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's the thing that's saying, let be the past and the future.

1091
01:19:25,194 --> 01:19:26,235
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll teach you the dhamma.

1092
01:19:27,135 --> 01:19:28,536
[SPEAKER_00]: With this, this is.

1093
01:19:29,876 --> 01:19:36,019
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, because as we spoke before, your past is present.

1094
01:19:37,000 --> 01:19:38,900
[SPEAKER_01]: Your future is present.

1095
01:19:40,681 --> 01:19:44,303
[SPEAKER_01]: You can think about your past at any given time, and you're doing it in the present.

1096
01:19:46,684 --> 01:19:48,685
[SPEAKER_01]: You can think ahead, plan your future,

1097
01:19:49,955 --> 01:19:51,937
[SPEAKER_01]: And you're thinking that, right there and then, in the present.

1098
01:19:52,538 --> 01:19:54,620
[SPEAKER_01]: Or you can think about present things in the present.

1099
01:19:56,202 --> 01:20:01,228
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have past, present, future, and they're all within the domain of present.

1100
01:20:01,428 --> 01:20:02,389
[SPEAKER_01]: And there's no choice about that.

1101
01:20:03,871 --> 01:20:10,038
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when the Buddha talks about present, he talks about that general domain of present, in which past, future, and present are present.

1102
01:20:11,167 --> 01:20:20,351
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't talk about that little present that's in between past and future, whereby don't think about the past, don't fantasize about the future, just be in the present.

1103
01:20:20,671 --> 01:20:21,312
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't help.

1104
01:20:22,752 --> 01:20:24,973
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's not really past-future-present.

1105
01:20:25,554 --> 01:20:29,475
[SPEAKER_01]: It's either past-present, present-future.

1106
01:20:29,495 --> 01:20:31,076
[SPEAKER_01]: Sorry, past-present or future-present.

1107
01:20:32,957 --> 01:20:37,539
[SPEAKER_01]: So it's over-determined and under-determined in regard to the determined, which is present.

1108
01:20:38,940 --> 01:20:41,361
[SPEAKER_01]: The linear of past, future, present, it's a fantasy.

1109
01:20:44,162 --> 01:20:50,585
[SPEAKER_01]: We're always like, in regard to present, things that were overly determined, things that were gone, things that were done, that's your past.

1110
01:20:51,805 --> 01:20:58,108
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, if you look at the other, the opposite way, in regard to this present, things that are yet to be, that's your future.

1111
01:20:58,728 --> 01:21:00,389
[SPEAKER_01]: But both are already within the present.

1112
01:21:03,890 --> 01:21:07,332
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can't stop thinking about the past or planning about the future.

1113
01:21:08,353 --> 01:21:14,703
[SPEAKER_01]: What you can do is, basically, not forget or not ignore that you're doing it presently.

1114
01:21:16,165 --> 01:21:17,287
[SPEAKER_00]: But you want to see the principle.

1115
01:21:17,888 --> 01:21:19,450
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that is already the principle.

1116
01:21:20,552 --> 01:21:21,293
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

1117
01:21:21,493 --> 01:21:22,314
[SPEAKER_01]: That's already the principle.

1118
01:21:24,020 --> 01:21:36,303
[SPEAKER_01]: And especially when it comes down to dhamma, as a teaching, if you talk about the principle of paticampāda, which is the timeless principle, principle of a kalika, that's a simultaneous presence.

1119
01:21:36,383 --> 01:21:37,364
[SPEAKER_01]: With this, this is.

1120
01:21:38,364 --> 01:21:40,645
[SPEAKER_01]: So, with appearance, existence is.

1121
01:21:41,005 --> 01:21:41,825
[SPEAKER_01]: What we spoke before.

1122
01:21:42,245 --> 01:21:43,825
[SPEAKER_01]: But with craving, suffering is.

1123
01:21:44,186 --> 01:21:46,546
[SPEAKER_01]: Simultaneously present, to the same extent.

1124
01:21:47,106 --> 01:21:49,147
[SPEAKER_01]: So now... and that is very important.

1125
01:21:49,832 --> 01:21:54,114
[SPEAKER_01]: To bring down and start discerning it together simultaneously to the same extent.

1126
01:21:54,154 --> 01:21:54,354
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

1127
01:21:54,394 --> 01:21:57,616
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you say suffering is because of craving.

1128
01:21:57,736 --> 01:22:06,920
[SPEAKER_01]: People immediately assume, naturally assume, it's the craving that arises and then endures in time for a while.

1129
01:22:06,980 --> 01:22:10,882
[SPEAKER_01]: They say, oh, craving, I crave, I'm doing this, and then the suffering follows.

1130
01:22:11,563 --> 01:22:11,763
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

1131
01:22:12,483 --> 01:22:13,564
[SPEAKER_00]: May I just crave a little bit?

1132
01:22:13,584 --> 01:22:16,625
[SPEAKER_01]: The suffering is in the very presence of craving.

1133
01:22:16,985 --> 01:22:18,106
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what suffering is.

1134
01:22:19,049 --> 01:22:23,893
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you could say it's the same thing, but it's not, because it can be discerned.

1135
01:22:25,134 --> 01:22:28,857
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's certainly simultaneously present things.

1136
01:22:31,119 --> 01:22:42,549
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can't possibly disjoin them and just say craving and look at the craving without suffering being present at the time.

1137
01:22:43,555 --> 01:22:49,138
[SPEAKER_01]: Nor can you be thinking about suffering and discerning suffering without craving being present at the time.

1138
01:22:50,538 --> 01:22:51,739
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's timeless.

1139
01:22:52,759 --> 01:23:02,024
[SPEAKER_01]: And no matter how much time externally might be passing, notion of time might be passing, yesterday to today, this principle of simultaneous present is the present.

1140
01:23:03,444 --> 01:23:09,327
[SPEAKER_01]: It's that general present which has past as over-determined and future as under-determined in regard to the determined.

1141
01:23:17,059 --> 01:23:26,166
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, but if you think that maybe it's craving and then suffering will arise in the future, there's maybe a possibility that it might not arise in the future.

1142
01:23:26,186 --> 01:23:26,466
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

1143
01:23:26,486 --> 01:23:27,407
[SPEAKER_00]: Something else might arise.

1144
01:23:27,467 --> 01:23:27,887
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

1145
01:23:28,187 --> 01:23:29,288
[SPEAKER_00]: So, therefore, craving is not...

1146
01:23:29,809 --> 01:23:35,933
[SPEAKER_01]: Therefore, craving is not the timeless determination of the suffering, which is what the Tathāgata says it is.

1147
01:23:36,254 --> 01:23:36,434
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

1148
01:23:36,854 --> 01:23:40,557
[SPEAKER_01]: With craving, suffering is.

1149
01:23:40,697 --> 01:23:41,918
[SPEAKER_00]: You might want to learn how to crave.

1150
01:23:42,898 --> 01:23:44,119
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, exactly, yeah.

1151
01:23:44,259 --> 01:23:44,999
[SPEAKER_00]: And not suffer.

1152
01:23:45,019 --> 01:23:52,224
[SPEAKER_01]: But you recognize, okay, so it's the presence of craving, results in the presence of suffering, right there and then, to the same extent.

1153
01:23:52,684 --> 01:23:58,708
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if the craving ceases, suffering cannot remain even for a nanosecond afterwards, without the suffering.

1154
01:23:59,208 --> 01:24:02,750
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's simultaneously arising and simultaneously ceasing.

1155
01:24:03,211 --> 01:24:05,172
[SPEAKER_01]: And that is the recognition of bodhichitta and pada.

1156
01:24:06,995 --> 01:24:25,821
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, obviously, if you have the ultimate, the greatest wisdom of the Saṅgha, Sambuddha, then that one principle of simultaneous arising, simultaneous ceasing, you can sort of describe and define many, many, many different, well, twelve or more in Digha Nikaya.

1157
01:24:26,610 --> 01:24:30,251
[SPEAKER_01]: When you just start describing paticcaṁpāda in slightly different terms later on, and so on.

1158
01:24:30,711 --> 01:24:33,672
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can go in all this intricately, but it's still the same principle.

1159
01:24:33,692 --> 01:24:37,913
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why every paticcaṁpāda starts with this, this is, and then with what?

1160
01:24:37,973 --> 01:24:38,753
[SPEAKER_01]: And then it goes on.

1161
01:24:39,714 --> 01:24:40,854
[SPEAKER_01]: It's within that principle.

1162
01:24:41,354 --> 01:24:49,376
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why... and that... and... yeah, he did say, one who sees... no, Sariputra said, one who sees dhamma sees paticcaṁpāda, one who sees paticcaṁpāda sees the dhamma, because that

1163
01:24:56,959 --> 01:24:58,320
[SPEAKER_01]: Principle of simultaneous presence.

1164
01:24:59,960 --> 01:25:08,363
[SPEAKER_01]: And you can... Again, you don't have to go into all of the intricate, very, very subtle descriptions of the Buddha and Bodhichitta Upādāna.

1165
01:25:09,524 --> 01:25:12,385
[SPEAKER_01]: You can just take the very coarse division of mind and body.

1166
01:25:13,946 --> 01:25:16,247
[SPEAKER_01]: Because those are general phenomena, real as such.

1167
01:25:17,667 --> 01:25:24,810
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you start seeing your body as, well, inconceivable, without the presence of the mind,

1168
01:25:25,993 --> 01:25:26,773
[SPEAKER_01]: And vice versa.

1169
01:25:27,653 --> 01:25:29,294
[SPEAKER_01]: The mind would be inconceivable.

1170
01:25:29,314 --> 01:25:31,134
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you might... like... and that's it.

1171
01:25:31,394 --> 01:25:39,155
[SPEAKER_01]: By not thinking about it, by not discerning it as inconceivable, you end up conceiving it through your assumption that it might stay on.

1172
01:25:39,836 --> 01:25:40,216
[SPEAKER_01]: It might.

1173
01:25:40,876 --> 01:25:44,496
[SPEAKER_01]: My mind might stay on when the body breaks apart.

1174
01:25:47,817 --> 01:25:52,738
[SPEAKER_01]: You conceive... many people conceive that through the assumption of it might.

1175
01:25:52,758 --> 01:25:53,178
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's it.

1176
01:25:54,619 --> 01:25:55,499
[SPEAKER_01]: What you just said before.

1177
01:25:56,000 --> 01:26:04,542
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't necessarily know, but just the fact that you don't see it simultaneously, you allow the possibility for it to enter, and that possibility is more than enough for the assumption of, yes, it will.

1178
01:26:07,503 --> 01:26:14,165
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you were to think long enough, and you realize, well, whenever you think about your mind, there is an image of the body, at least in the background, or at least right here, right now.

1179
01:26:14,925 --> 01:26:21,307
[SPEAKER_01]: If this body, physical body, is not operating, if this heart is not pumping, if this brain is not working,

1180
01:26:22,201 --> 01:26:23,121
[SPEAKER_01]: You wouldn't be able to think.

1181
01:26:23,861 --> 01:26:24,081
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

1182
01:26:24,122 --> 01:26:34,844
[SPEAKER_01]: Because any experience of thinking that you know of, that you can think of, that you can imagine, even in the future, implies the notion of your body.

1183
01:26:35,464 --> 01:26:38,325
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, it's inseparable from its saṅkhāra.

1184
01:26:39,906 --> 01:26:47,928
[SPEAKER_01]: And same again, if you were to start thinking about the body, you realize, well, I'm thinking about it, thus, I'm mentally accessing it, so to speak.

1185
01:26:48,008 --> 01:26:49,088
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's the only way you can.

1186
01:26:52,482 --> 01:26:53,323
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no pure sense.

1187
01:26:53,343 --> 01:26:54,864
[SPEAKER_01]: Sense in itself is not aware of itself.

1188
01:26:54,944 --> 01:26:55,825
[SPEAKER_01]: That's... it's not.

1189
01:26:56,965 --> 01:26:59,107
[SPEAKER_01]: But with the mind, there is sense.

1190
01:27:00,088 --> 01:27:00,828
[SPEAKER_01]: They meet in it.

1191
01:27:01,009 --> 01:27:01,769
[SPEAKER_01]: They appear in it.

1192
01:27:36,330 --> 01:27:38,972
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm beginning to see the extent of possibilities.

1193
01:27:39,552 --> 01:27:39,953
[SPEAKER_01]: So?

1194
01:27:40,173 --> 01:27:42,154
[SPEAKER_00]: To be able to see the extent of possibilities.

1195
01:27:42,174 --> 01:27:42,915
[SPEAKER_00]: What do you mean by that?

1196
01:27:43,955 --> 01:27:45,737
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, things can only go so far.

1197
01:27:46,177 --> 01:27:50,320
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, it can only go so... it can only be so far.

1198
01:27:51,441 --> 01:27:51,961
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I'm not...

1199
01:27:51,981 --> 01:27:52,382
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm not sure.

1200
01:27:52,402 --> 01:27:54,063
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, the mind is not...

1201
01:27:55,395 --> 01:27:56,355
[SPEAKER_00]: Not everything is possible.

1202
01:27:56,555 --> 01:27:58,716
[SPEAKER_00]: It can't... it can't survive by itself.

1203
01:27:58,856 --> 01:28:00,057
[SPEAKER_01]: Ah, OK. Yeah, yeah, yeah.

1204
01:28:00,137 --> 01:28:01,037
[SPEAKER_01]: OK. That's what you mean.

1205
01:28:01,057 --> 01:28:01,457
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, sure.

1206
01:28:01,618 --> 01:28:03,518
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

1207
01:28:03,558 --> 01:28:04,439
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't... by extent...

1208
01:28:04,459 --> 01:28:06,460
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't mean by the extent of all the possible... No.

1209
01:28:06,680 --> 01:28:09,001
[SPEAKER_01]: Basically, what you're saying is seeing the nature of possibilities.

1210
01:28:09,841 --> 01:28:10,521
[SPEAKER_01]: Seeing the nature.

1211
01:28:10,741 --> 01:28:15,403
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, you have to have a reasoned thing in order for possibilities to be possible in regard to what has a reason.

1212
01:28:15,503 --> 01:28:17,804
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, possibilities in themselves cannot exist.

1213
01:28:18,384 --> 01:28:18,524
Yeah.

1214
01:28:19,225 --> 01:28:24,427
[SPEAKER_01]: They cannot remain standing if there is not that which are possible... what they are possible in regard to.

1215
01:28:29,592 --> 01:28:30,493
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's the thing.

1216
01:28:30,513 --> 01:28:36,996
[SPEAKER_01]: You can assume the possibility that, you don't know, your mind might carry on, your consciousness might carry on itself.

1217
01:28:37,396 --> 01:28:45,480
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you stop and think about it intelligently, authentically, recognize that any notion, any designation of your mind, of your consciousness, is known in this experience as such.

1218
01:28:45,860 --> 01:28:51,943
[SPEAKER_01]: And here, when you think about the future, after your death, so to speak, you are assuming some sort of material body.

1219
01:28:52,787 --> 01:28:53,087
[SPEAKER_01]: You are.

1220
01:28:53,187 --> 01:28:59,510
[SPEAKER_01]: There is, like, not voluntarily, but it's implied in the notion of mind continuing.

1221
01:28:59,530 --> 01:28:59,811
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

1222
01:28:59,831 --> 01:29:04,073
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you only know mind as inseparable from, because it is inseparable from.

1223
01:29:05,133 --> 01:29:16,499
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, the question is basically just... sorry, no, the task is then to discern the notions of physical body that you carry in the assumptions of your mind in the future.

1224
01:29:17,701 --> 01:29:22,623
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, the assumptions of the mind as free from body and everything else will just cease.

1225
01:29:27,405 --> 01:29:32,747
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's inconceivable, even in theory, to just think about the mind without the body.

1226
01:29:34,447 --> 01:29:35,668
[SPEAKER_01]: Even that should affect it.

1227
01:29:36,648 --> 01:29:38,869
[SPEAKER_01]: By that principle of being separable from.

1228
01:29:39,449 --> 01:29:41,150
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you look at it closely, you'll see.

1229
01:29:41,711 --> 01:29:48,374
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, if you think my mind in the future, what you are there assuming most likely, most often, as your body, is basically your own face.

1230
01:29:48,774 --> 01:29:50,335
[SPEAKER_01]: Face of your own physical image.

1231
01:29:51,236 --> 01:29:55,498
[SPEAKER_01]: That you associate with that notion of mine, that it's now implied in that my mind.

1232
01:29:56,658 --> 01:30:02,301
[SPEAKER_01]: My mind, but at the very end of that image of my mind, there's a picture of pañña standing.

1233
01:30:03,242 --> 01:30:03,522
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

1234
01:30:04,225 --> 01:30:15,615
[SPEAKER_01]: If you push it far enough, you'll see, oh, you apply, you imply your current form in that notion of my mind, because mind is inseparable from the form, even in theory.

1235
01:30:17,417 --> 01:30:18,979
[SPEAKER_00]: Or sense of mind, sense of form.

1236
01:30:19,439 --> 01:30:23,843
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, well, that's the thing, that sense of mind or sense of form stands for that which form is.

1237
01:30:24,363 --> 01:30:30,289
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't actually have to have the physical physical, because even the physical physical is only a form of a sense of this current present.

1238
01:30:34,594 --> 01:30:35,374
[SPEAKER_01]: Currently, yes.

1239
01:30:39,696 --> 01:30:43,058
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, as I said, if you try and think about your body right here, right now, look at it.

1240
01:30:44,338 --> 01:30:49,161
[SPEAKER_01]: You know it, and aspects of it, because they appear mentally.

1241
01:30:49,881 --> 01:30:51,082
[SPEAKER_01]: These images appear mentally.

1242
01:30:52,562 --> 01:30:54,823
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus form rūpa in itself.

1243
01:30:54,923 --> 01:30:55,924
[SPEAKER_01]: It's inconceivable.

1244
01:30:56,904 --> 01:30:58,485
[SPEAKER_01]: But you can see it through the assumption.

1245
01:31:05,014 --> 01:31:06,695
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the nature of seeing assets.

1246
01:31:06,955 --> 01:31:08,536
[SPEAKER_00]: How it is simultaneously risen.

1247
01:31:08,976 --> 01:31:10,017
[SPEAKER_01]: Simultaneously risen.

1248
01:31:10,037 --> 01:31:14,159
[SPEAKER_01]: So, again, as I said, you don't have to... you don't have to go into intricate details of this and that.

1249
01:31:14,179 --> 01:31:17,700
[SPEAKER_01]: Just discover the principle where this, this is.

1250
01:31:19,401 --> 01:31:21,222
[SPEAKER_01]: And then drill everything through that.

1251
01:31:24,804 --> 01:31:26,425
[SPEAKER_00]: That's your gateway, that principle.

1252
01:31:29,366 --> 01:31:32,208
[SPEAKER_01]: Presence of the mind, presence of... Look, two separate domains.

1253
01:31:33,018 --> 01:31:36,059
[SPEAKER_01]: They exist... You don't want to say co-exist or anything like that.

1254
01:31:36,079 --> 01:31:36,299
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

1255
01:31:36,719 --> 01:31:38,099
[SPEAKER_01]: They appear simultaneous.

1256
01:31:39,180 --> 01:31:48,002
[SPEAKER_01]: And they're, like, impossible to discern one apart from the other.

1257
01:31:49,102 --> 01:31:54,424
[SPEAKER_01]: So even when you're just thinking abstractly about one, the other one is implying that thought.

1258
01:31:59,667 --> 01:32:09,737
[SPEAKER_01]: Or you can come down even slightly coarser, and you can even say, well, would you be able to think about a future and your mind carrying on, if this body is not living right here, right now?

1259
01:32:09,777 --> 01:32:14,662
[SPEAKER_01]: Would you be able to have those thoughts that pertain to the future, if this body is not breathing right there, while you're thinking it?

1260
01:32:15,362 --> 01:32:15,823
[SPEAKER_01]: You wouldn't.

1261
01:32:17,044 --> 01:32:21,728
[SPEAKER_01]: So, no matter how separate, how different it feels, it arranges together.

1262
01:32:21,768 --> 01:32:22,930
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why it's hard to see it.

1263
01:32:23,924 --> 01:32:27,025
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it only pertains to the simple nature of simultaneous presence.

1264
01:32:27,146 --> 01:32:28,346
[SPEAKER_01]: Nothing else in common.

1265
01:32:28,366 --> 01:32:35,390
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, it's so easy to assume that there are, mind and body, they might... they're two different entities.

1266
01:32:35,530 --> 01:32:36,590
[SPEAKER_01]: They're two separate entities.

1267
01:32:36,630 --> 01:32:39,932
[SPEAKER_01]: They're like, you know, they might sort of... one is left, the other one is right.

1268
01:32:39,952 --> 01:32:42,253
[SPEAKER_01]: They might do things together or whatever else.

1269
01:32:44,014 --> 01:32:50,497
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's hard to just see, to discern, that the only thing they really have in common is that they're simultaneously present.

1270
01:32:51,493 --> 01:32:53,074
[SPEAKER_01]: And you can't see one without the other.

1271
01:32:53,754 --> 01:32:54,894
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you build on that.

1272
01:33:00,036 --> 01:33:09,459
[SPEAKER_01]: Because no matter how pleasing your fantasy about, you know, well, my mind, my consciousness will continue because it makes sense, because of this, because of that.

1273
01:33:09,779 --> 01:33:13,820
[SPEAKER_01]: You're thinking those very things right there, right now, in that very present moment, while your body is breathing.

1274
01:33:14,060 --> 01:33:17,741
[SPEAKER_01]: Their body, their heart stops at the time, right there and then.

1275
01:33:18,081 --> 01:33:19,402
[SPEAKER_01]: Would you be able to continue those thoughts?

1276
01:33:22,180 --> 01:33:31,723
[SPEAKER_01]: When the only thoughts you know so far, and the thoughts that you're trying to send into future as valid facts, are known because of the breathing body right here or now.

1277
01:33:32,944 --> 01:33:40,227
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you actually have no concept of what experience of mind without a living body would be.

1278
01:33:40,247 --> 01:33:47,049
[SPEAKER_01]: And even when you think about the possibility of it, that already implies that body living, because you're thinking about the possibility of it while you're living.

1279
01:33:48,390 --> 01:33:49,550
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can't step outside of it.

1280
01:33:51,096 --> 01:33:52,324
[SPEAKER_01]: Unfortunately, you don't need to.

1281
01:33:53,868 --> 01:33:55,129
[SPEAKER_01]: All you have to do is stop assuming it.

1282
01:33:55,910 --> 01:34:00,393
[SPEAKER_01]: See one depending on the other and you, your sense of self, is completely redundant.

1283
01:34:00,794 --> 01:34:08,440
[SPEAKER_01]: And because it's redundant, it will fade by itself through the cultivation of this simultaneous... through this principle of simultaneity.

1284
01:34:08,460 --> 01:34:09,141
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's important.

1285
01:34:09,521 --> 01:34:15,606
[SPEAKER_01]: So you don't have to see your mind as inseparable from the body and the body as indistinguishable without the mind.

1286
01:34:16,447 --> 01:34:21,210
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't have to see that first and then free yourself from the self-view and I am.

1287
01:34:21,371 --> 01:34:21,551
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

1288
01:34:22,764 --> 01:34:25,045
[SPEAKER_01]: Freedom from those things will be the result of seeing that principle.

1289
01:34:28,806 --> 01:34:31,607
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the body is there given a reason and the mind in regard to it.

1290
01:34:33,747 --> 01:34:35,068
[SPEAKER_01]: There's no room for yourself.

1291
01:34:39,589 --> 01:34:44,790
[SPEAKER_01]: And by knowing that there can't be any sense of self in regard to those things, because they... they're indifferent to it.

1292
01:34:44,970 --> 01:34:45,951
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just mind and body.

1293
01:34:46,471 --> 01:34:47,711
[SPEAKER_01]: Two sticks holding each other.

1294
01:34:49,232 --> 01:34:50,612
[SPEAKER_01]: You remove one, the other one goes with it.

1295
01:34:52,360 --> 01:34:59,544
[SPEAKER_01]: By not knowing that you can't own those things, can't control, can't be in them, you place yourself in them.

1296
01:35:00,225 --> 01:35:00,665
[SPEAKER_01]: You assume.

1297
01:35:01,385 --> 01:35:02,066
[SPEAKER_01]: You assume you are.

1298
01:35:03,567 --> 01:35:06,869
[SPEAKER_01]: By fully discerning that you can't be there, you stop assuming that you are there.

1299
01:35:07,389 --> 01:35:08,670
[SPEAKER_01]: Because now you know that you can't be there.

1300
01:35:13,953 --> 01:35:20,857
[SPEAKER_01]: So this mind, right here, right there, and this body, right here, right there, whatever, for you, yours, for me, mine, is present.

1301
01:35:22,262 --> 01:35:33,029
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you can have all the, you know, on a more particular level, all the experiences of this sense or that sense, this emotion or that emotion, this aggregate or that aggregate, depending on the approach you have.

1302
01:35:33,270 --> 01:35:40,214
[SPEAKER_01]: But basically, you do need all the living body and the consciousness in regard to it.

1303
01:35:42,476 --> 01:35:43,337
[SPEAKER_01]: So, those two things.

1304
01:35:43,717 --> 01:35:44,858
[SPEAKER_01]: And they're inaccessible to you.

1305
01:35:48,000 --> 01:35:49,421
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't uproot your consciousness.

1306
01:35:50,517 --> 01:35:57,018
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what you can do is remove desire and lust in regard to them, and then there won't be any problems.

1307
01:36:17,477 --> 01:36:22,061
[SPEAKER_01]: Desire and lust that manifests through basically lust or aversion or delusion.

1308
01:36:23,582 --> 01:36:31,650
[SPEAKER_01]: In regard to that inaccessible, in regard to the principle of those inaccessible things, matter and consciousness.

1309
01:36:34,332 --> 01:36:39,457
[SPEAKER_01]: And when I say inaccessible, I mean, by that I mean you can't take all of it.

1310
01:36:39,617 --> 01:36:43,000
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't, I mean, you can't even think consciousness.

1311
01:36:44,348 --> 01:36:49,691
[SPEAKER_01]: You certainly can't think pure form, because you can see a contradiction.

1312
01:36:49,751 --> 01:36:51,892
[SPEAKER_01]: Thinking pure form, thus, that's a thought.

1313
01:36:51,952 --> 01:36:52,852
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not pure form.

1314
01:36:53,493 --> 01:36:58,975
[SPEAKER_01]: And even the idea of something being a pure form can only be known as a thought of that which pure form might be.

1315
01:36:59,075 --> 01:37:00,576
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, it's a thought in its nature.

1316
01:37:01,657 --> 01:37:03,358
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how can I know pure form?

1317
01:37:03,718 --> 01:37:04,518
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't.

1318
01:37:04,538 --> 01:37:09,100
[SPEAKER_01]: You can just remove its footing, its appropriation, so it can't touch you and you're done.

1319
01:37:09,321 --> 01:37:10,101
[SPEAKER_01]: That's all you need to do.

1320
01:37:18,442 --> 01:37:27,030
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the Buddha says he trains himself thus.

1321
01:37:27,931 --> 01:37:29,112
[SPEAKER_01]: Just training, repeating.

1322
01:37:29,172 --> 01:37:29,972
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what training is.

1323
01:37:30,072 --> 01:37:30,613
[SPEAKER_01]: Repetition.

1324
01:37:31,734 --> 01:37:35,357
[SPEAKER_01]: Just repeating, training, and training, and increasing, and training, and so on.

